(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m1-n93"]={"id":"n93","label":"Notes 9.3 · clearest slide-by-slide notes","revision":"ling406-modern-m1-n93-2","scripts":{"m1-lecture01-p001":[],"m1-lecture01-p002":[],"m1-lecture01-p003":[],"m1-lecture01-p004":[],"m1-lecture01-p005":[],"m1-lecture01-p006":[],"m1-lecture01-p007":[{"text":"**Letters** belong to a writing system. **Speech sounds** belong to the spoken language. So letters are not **phonemes**.","children":["Each language has its own rules for turning spelling into sound.","A familiar letter can lead an English reader to the wrong pronunciation in another language."]},{"text":"**English** spelling is especially ambiguous, or irregular. Even less irregular writing systems cause problems when languages are compared.","children":[]},{"text":"**Pinyin** is a relatively recently invented system for writing Mandarin. It is **perfectly regular**.","children":["For the Mandarin phrase \"Wo qu tushuguan\" (我去图书馆), the Pinyin letters look familiar to an English reader. English spelling rules still do not give the right pronunciation.","The Chinese characters and the Pinyin write the same Mandarin phrase in two different writing systems."]},{"text":"**French** spelling is also very regular.","children":["For the French sentence \"Comment tu t’appelles?\", the letters are again familiar. The reader still has to know the French rules to say it."]},{"text":"A writing system can be regular inside one language without being a sound code for every language. That is why comparing speech sounds has to start from what speakers actually say, not from matching letters.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p008":[{"text":"The **International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA)** is a **standard system** for writing the sounds of the world's languages. It accurately captures how sounds are **similar** and how they **differ**.","children":["An IPA symbol stands for properties of pronunciation. It does not stand for the spelling habits of one language.","Two sounds with different ordinary spellings can be compared directly.","Two sounds that ordinary writing spells with the same letter can be told apart."]},{"text":"The **IPA consonant chart** arranges its symbols by how the sounds are produced.","children":["The rows include **plosive**, **nasal** and **fricative**.","The columns include **bilabial**, **alveolar** and **velar**.","Symbols in related positions stand for related ways of producing a sound."]},{"text":"So where a symbol sits tells how its sound relates to the sounds near it. That is why the consonant chart works as a system of comparisons and not only as a list of symbols to memorize.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p009":[{"text":"The IPA consonant chart is organized by three **features**. They are **place**, **manner** and **voicing**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Place** of articulation is where the vocal tract is constricted.","children":["Place runs across the chart from the front of the vocal tract to the back.","**Bilabial** sounds sit toward the left because their constriction is at the lips.","**Velar** sounds and sounds made farther back sit farther to the right."]},{"text":"**Manner** of articulation is how the airflow is constrained. It is the degree of closure.","children":["Manner runs down the chart from closed to open.","For a **plosive**, the closure is complete.","For a **fricative**, the passage is narrower but not closed."]},{"text":"**Voicing** is about when the vocal folds vibrate relative to the closure. Voicing can begin before or during the closure, or after it.","children":["Voicing appears as pairs of symbols in one position. The right-hand symbol is the voiced one.","The symbols p and b share the plosive row and the bilabial column."]},{"text":"So two consonants can match on two features and **differ on the third**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p010":[{"text":"**Features** describe how sounds are alike and different in a relatively precise and well-defined way.","children":[]},{"text":"**/p/** is plosive, bilabial and not voiced. **/b/** is plosive, bilabial and voiced.","children":["Both have the **same manner**, because both are plosives.","Both have the **same place**, because both are bilabial. The lips close and then release.","They **differ in voicing**. Voicing here is the timing of phonation relative to the release of the closure."]},{"text":"The difference between **/t/ and /d/** is the same kind of difference.","children":["That pair is made at a different place.","It still pairs a voiceless stop with a voiced stop."]},{"text":"So features allow general statements about groups of sounds. One such group is the **voiced stops**, such as /b/, /d/ and /g/. That is why each consonant does not have to be described separately.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p011":[{"text":"The contrast between **voiced** and **unvoiced** stops is produced differently across languages. It is also produced differently across contexts within one language. The IPA can write these differences compactly.","children":[]},{"text":"Three **phones** are told apart by their features.","children":["**[pʰ]** is plosive, bilabial, not voiced and **aspirated**.","**[p]** is plosive, bilabial, not voiced and not aspirated.","**[b]** is plosive, bilabial and voiced.","**Aspiration** is the puff of air after the closure is released. The raised h in [pʰ] marks it."]},{"text":"**In English**, at the start of a word, the first sound of \"peer\" is often the unvoiced aspirated stop [pʰ]. The first sound of \"beer\" is often the unvoiced unaspirated stop [p].","children":["A hand held in front of the mouth makes the difference in airflow easy to feel."]},{"text":"**In most varieties of French**, the contrast is really between [p] in \"pou\" and [b] in \"boue\".","children":[]},{"text":"So the labels voiced and unvoiced do not mean the same timing in every language. That is why the narrow IPA symbols keep these three pronunciations apart.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p012":[{"text":"**Slashes** and **square brackets** mark two kinds of transcription.","children":[]},{"text":"Slashes mark **broad transcription**. Broad transcription writes the **phonemes** of an utterance.","children":["/p/ and /b/ are broad labels.","/p/ is plosive, bilabial and not voiced. /b/ is plosive, bilabial and voiced.","Broad transcription keeps the distinctions that organize the words of the language."]},{"text":"Square brackets mark **narrow transcription**. Narrow transcription includes **as much detail as possible**.","children":["[pʰ], [p] and [b] are narrow forms.","[pʰ] adds that the stop is aspirated. [p] adds that it is not aspirated.","Narrow transcription can keep a difference such as aspiration even when it does not distinguish words."]},{"text":"There are two broad labels here and three narrow forms. Writing down more phonetic detail does not create more phonemes. That is why a **phone** and a **phoneme** are not the same thing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p013":[{"text":"**Phonetic features**, especially for consonants, are anchored to **how speech is produced**. **Place of articulation** names a physical constriction in the vocal tract.","children":["The lips can close together.","Different parts of the tongue can approach or touch different regions of the mouth.","These movements change where the outgoing air is blocked or narrowed."]},{"text":"Three places differ in which articulators make the constriction.","children":["For a **bilabial** constriction, the upper and lower lips close at the front of the mouth.","For an **alveolar** constriction, the tongue tip rises toward the ridge behind the upper teeth.","For a **velar** constriction, the back of the tongue rises toward the soft palate."]},{"text":"A complete closure can happen at several different places. So place is a separate feature from manner. That is why finding the place means locating the main constriction, not reading the written letter.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p014":[{"text":"Consonant features describe two things. One is the **route of the airflow**. The other is which **articulators** make the constriction.","children":[]},{"text":"A **nasal** consonant lets air out through the nose while the mouth is closed at some point.","children":["The passage between the mouth and the nose is open, so air keeps flowing despite the closure in the mouth.","For a nasal made at the lips, the lips are closed and the route to the nasal cavity is open.","So a nasal can share its place in the mouth with a stop and differ in the route of the air."]},{"text":"Two other places are at the front of the mouth, at the teeth.","children":["For a **labiodental** constriction, the lower lip meets the upper teeth.","For a **dental** constriction, the tongue is at the front teeth."]},{"text":"So describing a consonant takes two separate steps. The first step follows the airflow. The second step locates the constriction.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p015":[{"text":"**Vowels** are organized by **height**, by **front or back** tongue position and by **lip rounding**.","children":[]},{"text":"Height separates close vowels from open vowels.","children":["**Close** vowels tend to have a higher jaw position and a higher tongue than **open** vowels.","The vowel chart runs from close at the top to open at the bottom."]},{"text":"Front or back is about where the tongue is.","children":["The tongue is generally farther forward in the mouth for **front** vowels than for **back** vowels.","The vowel chart runs from front on the left to back on the right."]},{"text":"Rounding is the rounding of the lips.","children":["Where two symbols appear as a pair, the one on the right is the **rounded** vowel."]},{"text":"**/y/** is the vowel in the French word \"tu\" and the Mandarin word \"qu\" (去).","children":["/y/ can sound like /u/ to English speakers.","/i/ and /y/ sit in the same close front region and differ in rounding.","Making an /i/ and then rounding the lips changes the vowel toward /y/. The tongue does not move back as it would for /u/."]},{"text":"So a change in **lip shape** is separate from a change in **tongue position**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p016":[{"text":"A **vowel** category describes a pattern of articulation. It does not describe one fixed tongue shape.","children":[]},{"text":"The center of the **tongue body** gives one reference point for each vowel.","children":["The point captures one important difference among vowels.","A point cannot give the whole outline of the tongue.","Tongue outlines for different vowels can differ in more than the location of that point.","The whole vocal tract contributes to the sound, not the tongue alone."]},{"text":"Some vowels are very **dynamic**.","children":["The **diphthongs** in \"boat\" and \"bait\" move through time. They are not one held configuration.","A point also cannot give the path the tongue takes during such a vowel."]},{"text":"So a fixed tongue position describes only part of how a vowel is produced. That is why a dynamic vowel is understood by following how the configuration **changes over its duration**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p017":[{"text":"Speakers of the same language can differ in which **vowels** they distinguish. Even within American English, vowels are very salient and differ across **dialects**.","children":[]},{"text":"The two vowels in the lower back corner of the vowel chart are the test case. One is the rounded low back vowel /ɔ/. The other is the open back vowel /ɑ/.","children":["**For a speaker from the outer boroughs of New York City**, a mid-Atlantic dialect, the two vowels are different. So \"caught\" and \"cot\" are **not homophones**.","**For most people from other parts of the country**, the two vowels are not distinguished. So \"caught\" and \"cot\" are **homophones**."]},{"text":"Both kinds of speaker are using English. The difference is a systematic dialect pattern and not a pronunciation mistake.","children":[]},{"text":"So the set of vowels has to be worked out for a particular speech variety. That is why finding out whether a speaker has the contrast means listening to that speaker.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p018":[{"text":"The **lack of invariance problem** is that one speech category has different acoustic forms across speakers and contexts. Listeners understand speech anyway.","children":[]},{"text":"Dialect differences are a first case.","children":["Some people say \"Aaron\" and \"Erin\" with the same vowel. A listener who keeps the two vowels apart still understands them.","A listener can hear a speaker produce different vowels for some time and only notice once attention is drawn to it."]},{"text":"Measured vowels are a second case. Each spoken vowel can be placed by its **first formant (F1)** and its **second formant (F2)**. Both are frequencies in cycles per second.","children":["Many productions of one vowel **spread** over a wide region. They do not land on one point.","The regions for neighboring vowels **overlap**."]},{"text":"So a vowel cannot be identified by checking for one fixed pair of F1 and F2 values. That is why the problem asks how a listener gets a stable interpretation from a signal that varies. The problem comes up again and again.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p019":[{"text":"A **minimal pair** is two words whose difference in meaning depends on one speech sound in the same context. Minimal pairs are important evidence of **contrast**.","children":[]},{"text":"Two phones are **contrastive** when a minimal pair exists in which the difference between them results in a difference in meaning. Contrastive phones are examples of **different phonemes**.","children":[]},{"text":"The two low back vowels were established this way. It took two different words whose distinction depended on the difference between those vowels.","children":["**For a speaker who distinguishes the vowels**, \"caught\" and \"cot\" are a minimal pair. The consonants around the vowel stay the same, so the vowel alone separates the words.","**For a speaker who merges the vowels**, \"caught\" and \"cot\" sound the same. So that pair does not establish two vowel phonemes for that speaker."]},{"text":"So the evidence for a phoneme is a **difference in meaning**. Two pronunciations sounding different is not enough.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p020":[{"text":"**Minimal pairs** also give evidence that phonemic contrasts depend on a particular **feature**.","children":[]},{"text":"In English, three pairs of stops all differ on **voicing**.","children":["**/b/ and /p/** are both bilabial stops, and voicing is what differs.","**/t/ and /d/** are both alveolar stops, and voicing is what differs.","**/k/ and /g/** are both velar stops, and voicing is what differs."]},{"text":"Inside each pair, place and stop manner stay the same and voicing changes. Across the pairs, the place changes and the voiced and voiceless relation repeats. So one feature describes all three contrasts.","children":[]},{"text":"The evidence comes from comparing minimal pairs in each language.","children":["Two phones existing physically does not make them a phonemic contrast in every language.","A minimal pair ties a particular sound difference to a difference between words."]},{"text":"That is why voicing is known to matter for telling English words apart.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p021":[{"text":"**Allophones** are different phones that are different realizations of the **same phoneme**.","children":[]},{"text":"There is a great deal of variability in how phonemes are realized. Some of this variability is very **predictable**.","children":["The variation depends on the position of the sound and on its neighboring sounds.","So it is systematic and not random noise.","It does not change which word is being said."]},{"text":"The phrases \"pot that\", \"spot that\" and \"stop that\" put **/p/** in three contexts. They can be recorded in **Praat** and compared.","children":["**In \"pot\"**, /p/ is at the start of the word, before the vowel.","**In \"spot\"**, /p/ comes after /s/.","**In \"stop\"**, /p/ is at the end of the word."]},{"text":"In all three, the consonant is still a realization of /p/. Its release and aspiration can differ from one context to the next. So the question is how pronunciation changes with context, not whether each difference makes a new word.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p022":[{"text":"A recording of \"pot that\", \"spot that\" and \"stop that\" makes the **allophones of /p/** observable in time.","children":[]},{"text":"The recording is read in two forms together.","children":["The **waveform** is on top. It gives the **amplitude** of the sound signal over time.","The **spectrogram** is below it. It gives the distribution of acoustic **energy** over time and frequency.","Time runs left to right in both, so the same event lines up vertically. Frequency runs up the side of the spectrogram."]},{"text":"The three phrases put /p/ in different contexts. So the timing of the **closure**, the **release**, the **aspiration** and the following voiced sound can differ.","children":["Abrupt releases and quieter intervals can be matched to the speech around them."]},{"text":"These physical differences fit with all three words containing the **same phoneme /p/**. Whether two phones are different phonemes depends on whether the difference distinguishes words in that language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p023":[{"text":"An unreleased **[p̚]** and an aspirated **[pʰ]** can both end the English word \"stop\" without changing its meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"English speakers sometimes say [stɑpʰ].","children":["This often happens when the word is stressed at the end of a sentence.","It also happens when someone is trying to speak clearly, in **citation form**."]},{"text":"There is **no difference in meaning** between [stɑp̚] and [stɑpʰ].","children":["No **minimal pair** can be found for these two phones in these phonological contexts in English.","So the two phones are called **allophones**. Allo means different, so allophones are different phones for the same phoneme."]},{"text":"These forms also alternate across the **morphological forms** of a word. The forms \"stop\", \"stops\", \"stopped\" and \"stopping\" each put something different after the /p/.","children":["Adding material after the consonant changes its environment.","So whether the /p/ is released and aspirated depends on which form the word is in."]},{"text":"That is why a difference that can be heard is not always a difference between phonemes.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p024":[{"text":"The word **voicing** is used in **two different ways**. That is why it can be confusing.","children":[]},{"text":"**In narrow transcription**, the description is of specific **phones**.","children":["A phone is called **voiced** if the vocal folds vibrate during the whole gesture or sound.","This is a claim about one actual production."]},{"text":"**In broad transcription**, the description is of **phonemes**.","children":["Voicing is **realized** in a number of different ways.","One way is the presence or absence of **aspiration** at the beginnings of words.","Another way is the relative duration of voicing to closure in \"writer\" and \"rider\".","This is a claim about a contrast between categories."]},{"text":"So one voicing contrast between phonemes can have more than one physical cue. The cue changes with the context. That is why the first thing to ask about the word voiced is which level is being described.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p025":[{"text":"**Phonotactics** is, roughly, which phonemes can occur together in syllable structures.","children":[]},{"text":"The rules are clearest with **clusters** that cannot occur in English words.","children":["The name Sbarro is written with sb at the start.","English speakers say [spɑroʊ] or [zbɑroʊ].","They **never** say [sbɑroʊ], whatever they think they are saying."]},{"text":"Foreign names can force speakers to deal with clusters that English phonotactics does not allow. Speakers then tend to **insert vowels**.","children":["**For the Polish name Zbigniew**, they say [zɨ’bIg.nʲu].","**For the Kru name Gbagbo**, they say [gə.bɑ.gə.boʊ].","The inserted vowels break the cluster into extra syllables."]},{"text":"These changes follow the patterns of English syllables. They do not come from being unable to make each single consonant.","children":[]},{"text":"Rules about syllable structure turn out to be complex. They still seem to shape **speech production**. So the spelling alone does not predict what an English speaker says.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p026":[{"text":"**Phonemes** are not letters. They are not even specific sounds or gestures.","children":[]},{"text":"A phoneme is \"the smallest phonetic unit in a language that is capable of conveying a distinction in meaning.\"","children":["So a phoneme is defined by the **contrasts** it makes between words. This ties it to **minimal pairs**.","One phoneme can have several **allophones** and still be one category.","Applying the definition can be complicated in practice."]},{"text":"Three things belong to three different descriptions.","children":["A **letter** is part of a writing system.","A **phone** is an actual speech realization.","A **phoneme** organizes the contrasts that carry meaning."]},{"text":"The **International Phonetic Alphabet** gives speech scientists a way of communicating about similarities and differences between sounds across languages.","children":["So it is easy to think of the IPA as an alphabet of phonemes.","That would be a mistake, because phonemes are not letters."]},{"text":"That is why the number of written symbols, phones and phonemes can all differ.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p027":[{"text":"Speech is a **continuously changing signal**. It is not an orderly stream of letter-like units.","children":[]},{"text":"Literate people easily imagine speech as a row of separate units like letters. It is nothing like that.","children":[]},{"text":"A **spectrogram** of the sentence \"A sore back is a real pain\" makes the difference visible.","children":["Time runs from left to right, and frequency increases upward.","Darker regions have stronger acoustic energy.","Broad frequency patterns change over time, and transitions connect neighboring segments.","The phonetic labels and boundary markers are added on top. They organize the reading of the signal and do not cut it into separate pieces."]},{"text":"A **phonemic transcription** gives useful categories. Those categories do not match isolated blocks that have the same acoustic shape in every context.","children":[]},{"text":"So recognizing the sentence means interpreting changing patterns. It does not mean finding a clean boundary around each letter. That is why **phonemes are not letters**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p028":[{"text":"Speech takes coordinated movements of the **articulators**. They are the lips, jaw, tongue and other structures of the vocal tract.","children":[]},{"text":"Each part changes the vocal tract in its own way.","children":["The **tongue** changes the internal shape of the oral cavity.","The **lips and jaw** change its opening.","Together, these positions shape the sound that comes out."]},{"text":"A side view of the vocal tract, from USC SPAN, makes the articulators visible in action.","children":["It locates the tongue inside the mouth and the passage extending toward the throat.","The outline of the lips and the position of the tongue link the spoken output to the shape of the vocal tract."]},{"text":"The movements run as one **ongoing sequence**. So a speech **gesture** is part of a moving system. It is not one posture repeated unchanged for every occurrence of a phoneme. That is why the articulators do not stamp out one letter-like sound at a time.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p029":[{"text":"**Hockett (1955)** gave an analogy for why recognizing speech is not recovering a row of separate pieces.","children":[]},{"text":"A row of Easter eggs is carried along a moving belt. The eggs have various sizes and colors and are not boiled.","children":["The flow of eggs before the wringer stands for the series of impulses from the **phoneme source**."]},{"text":"The belt carries the eggs between the two rollers of a wringer. The rollers smash the eggs and rub them more or less into each other.","children":["The mess that emerges stands for the output of the **speech transmitter**.","So information about neighboring elements is spread together in the speech signal."]},{"text":"An **inspector** then examines the passing mess. He uses the broken and unbroken yolks, the spread-out albumen and the colored bits of shell. He decides what flow of eggs arrived at the wringer.","children":["The inspector stands for the **hearer**.","He does not have to put the eggs together again, which is physically impossible. He only has to **identify** them."]},{"text":"So a hearer can identify the original sequence from a mixed signal without rebuilding each separate piece.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p030":[{"text":"People have started to question whether **phonemes** are \"real\", either psychologically or linguistically.","children":[]},{"text":"A phonemic description is useful for describing contrasts. That does not settle how a speaker stores words or how a listener recognizes speech.","children":["A descriptive notation answers one question.","A model of mental processing answers a related but different question."]},{"text":"Several papers raise the issue.","children":["**Lotto and Holt (2000)** wrote about the illusion of the phoneme.","**Beckman and Edwards (2000)** wrote about how phonological categories develop and the primacy of lexical learning.","**Port (2007)** asked how words are stored in memory, beyond phones and phonemes.","**Goldinger and Azuma (2003)** wrote about puzzle-solving science in speech perception."]},{"text":"What these papers share is the relation between the categories used for analysis and actual **cognitive representation**. So the open question is whether phoneme-sized units are the right description of the mental processes themselves.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture01-p031":[],"m1-lecture01-p032":[],"m1-lecture03-p001":[],"m1-lecture03-p002":[],"m1-lecture03-p003":[],"m1-lecture03-p004":[{"text":"A **word form** depends on the job the word does in its sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"The argument is over a Scrabble game. One player says knifes is not a word because the plural of knife is knives. The other player answers that Tony knifes Bernardo in West Side Story.","children":["**For the noun** knife, the plural is **knives**. That is **inflectional morphology**, which changes a word's form for grammar, as in plural formation.","**For the verb** knife, the form after he is **knifes**. The noun became a verb by **zero derivation**. Zero derivation is **derivational morphology** that changes the part of speech without adding an affix.","**In \"He knifes him\"**, the pattern is a singular noun, then a verb, then a noun. The singular subject requires the **third person singular** ending on the verb."]},{"text":"So each player is right about a different word. Knives is the noun's plural, and knifes is the verb form that the sentence requires.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p005":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology** is a process that derives new words from existing parts. The parts, or units, are called **morphemes**.","children":["A morpheme contributes meaning or grammatical information. It is not the same thing as a syllable or a single speech sound."]},{"text":"The word morphology has a fairly transparent derivation.","children":["**The part morph-** comes from Greek and means form.","**The part -ology** also comes from Greek and here means something like the study of.","Together they give \"the study of the forms of words\"."]},{"text":"A similar process gives the word **morpheme**.","children":["Phone is another morpheme from Greek, probably meaning speech. It gives **phoneme**, the unit of speech sound.","By the same pattern, a unit in text should get a name built the same way.","The pattern does not always work. Graphology looks like it should be the study of those text units, but that is not what the word means."]},{"text":"So a complex word can be taken apart into morphemes that appear again in different words.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p006":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology** is **productive**, which means it can make new words. It is productive only in limited ways.","children":["Made-up words are hard to guess, because the meaning of the final form is only **partially predictable**."]},{"text":"**Zero derivation** changes the part of speech of a word without adding anything. Even there, the new word usually works with a **narrow sense** of the base word.","children":["**For the noun** knife, many objects count. Image search results for knife include different cutting tools and tableware. A search for prison shiv gives an improvised weapon.","**For the verb** knife, only one kind of action counts. To knife somebody is to attack that person with a knife.","So the weapon-like objects, such as the prison shiv, fit the verb naturally. A table knife is still a knife, but it is not a natural fit for knifing somebody."]},{"text":"That is why changing a noun into a verb also takes knowledge of which sense the verb conventionally uses.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p007":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology** is also limited in how widely and how regularly **affixes** are applied. An affix is a part added to a stem, such as -hood.","children":[]},{"text":"The pattern is a stem plus **-hood**. It gives a state of being that is associated with the stem noun.","children":["**For brotherhood and sisterhood**, the pattern works as expected.","**For knighthood**, the result is a little odd. Two brothers share their brotherhood, but each knight has his own knighthood.","**For neighborhood**, the word is perfectly good, but -hood does a different job entirely. It does not mean the state of being a neighbor.","**For friendhood**, English says **friendship** instead, for no apparent reason.","**For enemyhood**, there is no such word, although maybe there ought to be. The nearest words are less common ones such as enmity or antagonism."]},{"text":"So a productive affix does not attach equally well to every stem that would make sense. Knowing an affix includes knowing which words actually use it.","children":["Some of these words come from Liberman and Prince's introductory linguistics course at Penn."]}],"m1-lecture03-p008":[{"text":"**Zero derivation** changes a word's category without adding a visible affix. Even so, it does complicated things with **semantics**, which is meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"Dish, spoon, fork and table are strong associates of the noun knife. Their meanings as verbs are **not similar** to each other.","children":["**In \"She can dish it out but she can’t take it\"**, dish is a verb. Its meaning is learned and does not come from naming the object.","**In \"He was born with a silver spoon and a forked tongue in his mouth\"**, forked takes the shape of a fork. Forked tongue also has an established figurative meaning.","**In \"They decided to table the issue after a day of inconclusive negotiations\"**, table is a verb with a learned meaning."]},{"text":"Reading these as literal references to utensils or furniture would miss the intended meanings.","children":[]},{"text":"So words that are closely related as nouns can become verbs with unrelated meanings. That is why the meaning after zero derivation depends on **conventional usage**.","children":["The silver spoon expression is the kind of thing studied in **Cognitive Linguistics**."]}],"m1-lecture03-p009":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology** more commonly changes the pronunciation and spelling of a word. One way is by adding **affixes**.","children":[]},{"text":"Adding an affix can change the **phonology** of the rest of the word. Phonology is the sound pattern of the word.","children":["**For certificate**, the pronunciation is /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/.","**For certification**, it is /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/.","**For recertification**, it is /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/.","The **stress** falls on different syllables across the three words, and some vowels change too."]},{"text":"This is one reason for \"The Chaos\" of English spelling. Spelling that follows the spoken form more closely does not necessarily make words easier to read.","children":[]},{"text":"Some noun and verb pairs differ mainly in **stress**.","children":["**For record**, the noun and the verb have the same spelling but different stress.","**For invite**, the same holds in \"He did not receive an invite\" and \"We did not invite him\".","**For entrance**, the noun and the verb also differ in stress. Those two words are not actually related to each other."]},{"text":"So a derived word is not always the base pronunciation with an ending attached.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p010":[{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** changes the meaning of words in a more **predictable** way than derivation does. It gives a different grammatical form of the same word.","children":[]},{"text":"**For plurals**, knife(n) becomes knives. One fork becomes two forks. One mouse becomes a lot of mice.","children":[]},{"text":"**For the third person singular present**, knife(v) becomes knifes. I walk becomes she walks. You run becomes he runs.","children":[]},{"text":"**For the past**, knife(v) becomes knifed. Today I walk becomes yesterday I walked. Today she sings becomes in the past, she sang.","children":[]},{"text":"Menn writes that “...we’d think it was silly to have separate dictionary entries for the singular and plural forms of nouns.”","children":["So inflected forms belong to one dictionary word and are not new words."]},{"text":"The phonological process of inflecting words often has some **irregularity**.","children":["Forks and walked use regular endings. Mice and sang change the sound of the word instead.","The grammatical meaning is the same in both kinds. Mice is still plural and sang is still past.","This irregularity matters for different language learning models and for kinds of developmental language impairment."]}],"m1-lecture03-p011":[{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** also depends on the **syntax** of the rest of the sentence. Syntax is how words are organized into phrases and sentences.","children":[]},{"text":"The phrase \"two [noun]+pl\" has a slot for a noun. Whatever noun goes there must be pluralized somehow.","children":["**For a regular noun**, the slot gives two forks.","**For an irregular noun**, the slot gives two knives.","The plural requirement stays the same whichever noun fills the slot."]},{"text":"**Derivational morphology** has no syntactic process like this, even for very productive forms.","children":["The closest thing is a formula such as “Once you [verb] it, you can un-[verb] it.”","That formula is not syntax. No sentence requires every verb to have an un- form."]},{"text":"So syntax can require an inflection, but it does not require a derivation.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p012":[{"text":"English is fairly limited in its use of **inflection**. Other languages mark much more grammatical information on their words.","children":[]},{"text":"English inflects for three things here.","children":["**For plurals**, there are knives, two forks and a lot of mice.","**For the third person singular present**, there are knifes, she walks and he runs.","**For the past**, there are knifed, walked and sang."]},{"text":"Many other languages do more.","children":["**For person**, many languages conjugate verbs much more elaborately than English does. The same is true for tense.","**For number**, many languages have different ways of forming the plural for small and large numbers.","**For nouns**, many languages have **case markings**. Case markings encode information about syntactic or semantic roles."]},{"text":"So English has inflection, but it does not have the full range found across languages.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p013":[{"text":"**Inflectional** and **derivational morphology** share two properties and differ in the rest.","children":[]},{"text":"**Both kinds** create new phonological forms out of old parts. Both kinds change the meanings of base forms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Derivational morphology** creates **new words**.","children":["It is productive, but not always, and its productivity is typically limited.","The verb knife made from the noun knife is derivational."]},{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** changes how words interact syntactically with other words.","children":["It is **highly productive**, but it is not always regular.","It is **required by syntax**.","The form knifes after he is inflectional."]},{"text":"So the test is what the change does. A change that makes a new word is derivation. A change that gives the form a sentence requires is inflection.","children":["Regular or irregular sound does not decide between them."]}],"m1-lecture03-p014":[{"text":"Sentences are built from more than single words. Words group into **phrases**, **clauses** and **sentences**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Content words** include nouns and verbs. Other content word classes are modifiers such as adjectives and adverbs.","children":["Parts of speech have come up only in a limited way before this point. That is because the cases were mostly single nouns and verbs."]},{"text":"**Function words** matter once more than a couple of words are put together. Determiners and prepositions are function words.","children":["They organize how the content words relate to each other."]},{"text":"The sentence “The video for “More than Words” came out before most of you were born” has two **noun phrases**.","children":["**The first noun phrase** is The video for “More than Words”.","**The second noun phrase** is most of you.","The two differ in length, so a noun phrase is not defined by a number of words."]},{"text":"So understanding a sentence takes more than knowing the meanings of its nouns and verbs.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p015":[{"text":"The **noun phrase** The video for “More than Words” has three parts. Its structure is a determiner, then a noun, then a prepositional phrase.","children":[]},{"text":"Two **function words** organize it.","children":["**The** is a **determiner**. It begins the noun phrase.","**For** is a **preposition**. It begins the prepositional phrase.","The **prepositional phrase** lets the song title specify which video is meant."]},{"text":"**Video** is the head noun of the phrase.","children":[]},{"text":"The title “More than Words” is not really part of the sentence here. It is being used like a noun.","children":["With the quotes, the title names the song as one unit.","Without the quotes, it is not a phrase. It is a bunch of words strung together in a row."]},{"text":"So this NP has the structure **Det + N + PP**. Det is the determiner, N is the noun, and PP is the prepositional phrase.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p016":[{"text":"The noun phrase structure **Det + N + PP** is incredibly **abstract**. Phrases with different words and lengths can share it.","children":[]},{"text":"Three phrases have the same structure.","children":["**For \"two guys from the early ‘nineties\"**, two is the determiner and guys is the noun. From the early ‘nineties is the modifier.","**For \"a band with only one hit\"**, a is the determiner and band is the noun. With only one hit is the modifier.","**For \"one and only one phrase unrelated to early ‘nineties one-hit wonder Extreme\"**, one and only one is the determiner. Phrase is the noun, and the rest is the modifier."]},{"text":"The parts can differ inside.","children":["A determiner can be several words, as in one and only one.","The first two modifiers are prepositional phrases that begin with from and with.","The third modifier begins with the adjective unrelated, followed by a to phrase. It is an adjective phrase with a prepositional phrase inside it."]},{"text":"So the structure names positions and categories, not particular words.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p017":[{"text":"An **abstract structure** such as noun phrase leaves out the individual words. This abstraction is needed for two reasons.","children":[]},{"text":"**Within one language**, it lets very different surface forms count as similar. Without it, there is no way to say that two phrases with different words are alike.","children":[]},{"text":"**Across languages**, it lets similarities and differences be stated coherently. All languages have **NPs**, but they can form them very differently.","children":["**For English**, the phrase is the cat in the coffee shop. The determiner comes first and the prepositional phrase follows the noun.","**For Chinese**, the phrase is 咖啡店裡面的小貓, which is coffeeshop inside + cat. The order is PP then N.","The Chinese NP has **no determiner**."]},{"text":"This helps explain why learning to use determiners in a second language (L2) is so difficult for speakers of Chinese languages. Their own NPs are formed without one, so the determiner has to be learned new.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p018":[{"text":"A **prepositional phrase** is a preposition followed by a noun phrase. Its general structure is **P + NP**.","children":[]},{"text":"Breaking down three prepositional phrases gives the same structure each time.","children":["**For \"from the early ‘nineties\"**, from is the P. The NP is the early ‘nineties, which is Det + Adj + N.","**For \"with only one hit\"**, with is the P. The NP is only one hit, which is given as P + Det + N.","**For \"unrelated to early ‘nineties one-hit wonder Extreme\"**, unrelated to is treated as the P. The NP is early ‘nineties one-hit wonder Extreme, given as Adv + Adj + Adv + Adj + N."]},{"text":"The NP inside can be short or long. The preposition relates that whole NP to the rest of the expression.","children":[]},{"text":"So all these prepositional phrases are just **P + NP**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p019":[{"text":"Phrase structure is **hierarchical**, which means phrases sit inside other phrases.","children":[]},{"text":"Consider the phrase from the early ‘nineties.","children":["The **determiner**, the **adjective** and the **noun** are all inside the **noun phrase**.","The noun phrase is inside the **prepositional phrase**.","The prepositional phrase is inside a sentence, or inside yet another noun phrase."]},{"text":"A flat row of words with curly brackets and parentheses is a clumsy way to write this. The row has one level, but the structure has several.","children":[]},{"text":"**Trees** handle the levels better. A tree gives each phrase a node above the parts it contains.","children":["That is partly why people started using trees to diagram sentences."]},{"text":"So words that sit side by side can belong to different levels of structure. Hierarchy is also an important feature of languages.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p020":[{"text":"A **verb phrase** is a verb together with the material that completes it. Verb phrases differ in what follows the verb.","children":[]},{"text":"In the sentence “The video for “More than Words” came out before most of you were born”, the verb phrase is V + PP.","children":["The verb is came out.","The PP is before most of you were born.","That PP is hard to unpack. Most of you were born looks like a verb phrase, but it specifies a point in time that complements before."]},{"text":"Many verb phrases also contain **noun phrases**.","children":["**For sang the song**, the structure is V + NP.","**For worked himself to death**, the structure is V + NP + PP. Himself is the NP and to death is the PP.","**For threw a party for him**, the structure is V + NP + PP. A party is the NP and for him is the PP."]},{"text":"So the verb is not the whole verb phrase. The phrases that follow the verb belong to it too.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p021":[{"text":"A **clause** is a verb phrase and its subject. The **subject** is the noun phrase that controls the verb.","children":[]},{"text":"The sentence divides into two parts.","children":["**The NP** is The video for “More than Words”. Its structure is Det + N + PP.","**The VP** is came out before most of you were born. Its structure is V + PP."]},{"text":"The clause is found by pairing the VP with its subject.","children":["The whole first NP is the **subject**.","The **head noun** of that NP is video.","The song title sits inside the PP, so it is not the subject."]},{"text":"So the whole sentence is **one clause**. It has one VP and one subject.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p022":[{"text":"The subject **controls** the verb, which means the verb agrees with the **head** of the subject noun phrase.","children":[]},{"text":"“The plums that were in the icebox were delicious” is a simple sentence with one clause.","children":["Its subject NP is The plums that were in the icebox, given as Det + N + PP. Its VP is were delicious.","The verb agrees with plums, not icebox, because plums is the head of the noun phrase."]},{"text":"“The plums in the icebox, which you were probably saving for breakfast, were delicious” has a **clause within a clause**. The inner clause is set off with commas.","children":["**For the inner clause**, you is the subject of were probably saving for breakfast.","**For the outer clause**, plums is the subject of were delicious.","Changing you to my roommate changes only the inner verb. It becomes was probably saving, and were delicious stays the same."]},{"text":"**In \"Forgive me\"**, the subject is **implicit**, which means it is not said. The speaker wants whoever shares his icebox to forgive him for eating the plums.","children":[]},{"text":"So each verb agrees with its own subject, not with the nearest noun.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p023":[{"text":"Two sentences can mean the same thing and still be very different **syntactically**.","children":[]},{"text":"**In \"I have eaten the plums in the icebox\"**, the subject comes first.","children":["The **subject** is I.","A verb phrase follows it.","The verb phrase includes the NP the plums in the icebox. That NP is the **object** of the verb."]},{"text":"**In \"The plums in the icebox were eaten by me\"**, the plums in the icebox becomes the **subject**.","children":["The evidence is agreement. The verb were eaten agrees with the plural noun plums.","The phrase by me is just a PP that fills out the VP. It names who did the eating, but it is not the subject."]},{"text":"The first sentence is **active** and the second is **passive**.","children":[]},{"text":"So the subject is the phrase that controls the verb. It is not always the one who did the action.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p024":[{"text":"**Semantic roles** say what each participant does in the event. **Syntactic roles** say what each phrase does in the sentence, such as subject or object.","children":[]},{"text":"The roles in the plum sentences are **agent**, **undergoer** and **location**. The agent is the eater, the undergoer is the plums, and the location is the icebox.","children":[]},{"text":"**For the passive sentence**, the order is Undergoer + Location + Verb + Agent.","children":["The plums is the undergoer, in the icebox is the location, and by me is the agent.","The subject is the undergoer."]},{"text":"**For the active sentence**, the order is Agent + Verb + Undergoer + Location.","children":["I is the agent, the plums is the undergoer, and in the icebox is the location.","The subject is the agent."]},{"text":"So the **semantic roles do not change** between the two sentences. Only the syntactic roles change.","children":["English usually aligns the syntactic subject with the agent. That is why it seems counterintuitive that me is not the subject of the passive sentence.","Grade school teaches that grammar tells \"who did what to whom\". Semantic roles do that job more than syntactic roles do.","The relationship between syntactic and semantic roles also influences sentence processing."]}],"m1-lecture03-p025":[{"text":"Language is highly **generative**, which means familiar parts can be combined into new words and new sentences.","children":[]},{"text":"Three processes make it generative.","children":["**Derivational morphology** makes new words.","**Syntax** organizes words into clauses, clauses into phrases, and phrases into sentences.","**Inflectional morphology** modifies words for different syntactic roles."]},{"text":"People do this mostly **without thinking explicitly** about it.","children":["A speaker can pick a plural or build a phrase without naming any grammatical category."]},{"text":"One of the hardest things to understand about linguistics is what there is to explain. That is because some of the most complex phenomena are hiding in plain sight.","children":[]},{"text":"So ordinary speaking uses derivation, inflection and syntax together, even though it feels effortless.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p026":[],"m1-lecture03-p027":[],"m1-lecture04-p001":[],"m1-lecture04-p002":[{"text":"**Association norms** summarize the words people produce in response to a cue word.","children":["A participant sees a word on a screen for a few seconds.","The participant writes down the first few words that come to mind."]},{"text":"The task asks for **immediate associations**, not a dictionary definition.","children":["The same cue can bring up different associates in different people.","Collecting responses across many people reveals which associations recur and which are unusual."]},{"text":"So a norm is a **pattern of responses** to each cue. It is measured through what people actually write.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p003":[{"text":"LION is a **cue** in the association task. The participant writes the first words that LION brings to mind.","children":[]},{"text":"The responses reveal connections in the participant's **lexical knowledge**, which is what a person knows about words.","children":["A response such as TIGER would connect two animal concepts.","TIGER is a possible associate, not a response that every participant must give."]},{"text":"So the cue only starts the response. The participant supplies the association.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p004":[{"text":"TRIPE is a cue whose **form** looks like STRIPES. The two words share a sequence of letters.","children":[]},{"text":"Shared letters do not mean a shared **meaning**.","children":["Similarity in form is one relationship.","A connection between meanings is a different relationship.","Overlapping letters can occur in words with very different meanings."]},{"text":"So TRIPE and STRIPES make the **arbitrariness** of form and meaning concrete. The spelling of a word does not tell you what it means.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p005":[{"text":"FREE is another cue in the association task.","children":["The participant supplies words that come to mind from this cue.","The participant does not choose from a list of answers."]},{"text":"The response is **generated** by the participant.","children":["Recording it makes an internal association available to study.","Responses can then be compared across people."]},{"text":"So the norm for FREE comes from what many participants produce on their own.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p006":[{"text":"STRIPES is a cue that names a **visual property**.","children":[]},{"text":"An association can connect a **property** with an object or animal that has it.","children":["STRIPES and TIGER can be associated.","One word names a pattern, and the other names an animal.","This link differs from the **similarity** between two animal concepts such as LION and TIGER."]},{"text":"So associated words do not have to name the same kind of thing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p007":[{"text":"PEAR is a cue whose **form** overlaps with BEAR.","children":[]},{"text":"The two words look alike but have different meanings.","children":["PEAR names a fruit.","BEAR names an animal.","Their resemblance in form does not make them the same concept."]},{"text":"So PEAR and BEAR separate a **formal relationship** from a **semantic relationship**. A shared sound or spelling pattern is not evidence of similar meaning.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p008":[{"text":"BEAR is another cue for a **freely generated association**.","children":[]},{"text":"Its form overlaps with PEAR.","children":["The two words resemble each other in form.","They do not resemble each other in meaning."]},{"text":"**Association norms** record the responses people actually give.","children":["The experimenter does not decide in advance whether a response will be based on form or meaning."]},{"text":"So the norm captures whatever connection the cue really brings to mind.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p009":[{"text":"TREE is a cue for the association task.","children":[]},{"text":"The participant's first responses give evidence about which words are **readily connected** with TREE.","children":["This evidence holds for that task context.","Different people can supply different associates for the same cue."]},{"text":"The **norm** summarizes all those responses.","children":["It does not treat one response as the word's only association."]},{"text":"So a cue stays the same physical word, while its associates vary across people.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p010":[{"text":"TIGER is a cue that connects to two other cues in different ways.","children":[]},{"text":"For **LION**, the link is **similar meaning**, because both are animals.","children":["For **STRIPES**, the link is a **characteristic pattern** that tigers have."]},{"text":"An animal can be associated with one of its properties.","children":["Two animal names can be related through shared category membership.","Both kinds of link can be part of the structure of a **lexical network**."]},{"text":"So **association** and **similarity** are not the same relationship.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p011":[],"m1-lecture04-p012":[{"text":"Three cues are tabulated as norms because they matter later. These are **LION**, **TIGER** and **STRIPES**.","children":[]},{"text":"The three cues connect meanings in different ways.","children":["LION and TIGER name **similar animals**.","TIGER and STRIPES connect an animal with a **characteristic property**.","Their associations can be measured by counting how often participants give one word in response to another."]},{"text":"The pairs TRIPE and STRIPES, and PEAR and BEAR, make a separate point.","children":["The two words in each pair overlap in form.","Similar form does not guarantee similar meaning.","The relationship between the sound or spelling of a word and its meaning is largely **arbitrary**."]},{"text":"So three relationships must be kept apart. These are similarity of form, similarity of meaning and the tendency of one word to bring another to mind.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p013":[{"text":"Coming up with associates raises questions about how the norms were produced.","children":[]},{"text":"Participants may have used a deliberate **strategy** to come up with associates.","children":["A strategy can change which words a person writes."]},{"text":"The **list structure** could influence the answers.","children":["A list of only wild animals could encourage one set of responses.","A list of only household appliances could encourage a different set.","The surrounding list changes which ideas are easy to bring to mind."]},{"text":"**Associated** and **similar** are different.","children":["**Association** is a tendency for one item to bring another to mind.","**Similarity** is about shared properties.","TIGER and STRIPES are associated, but they are not two similar objects."]},{"text":"So a norm is evidence about responses under specific conditions. Interpreting it means asking how the cue was presented and what other cues were present.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p014":[{"text":"Menn's chapter is titled \"How Brains Work\". A better title might be \"Some basic metaphors from neuroscience that are useful in psycholinguistics\".","children":[]},{"text":"These **metaphors from neuroscience** are the basis for understanding most theory and experimental design in the field.","children":[]},{"text":"Five ideas make up this vocabulary.","children":["**Activation** is the activity level of a representation.","**Spreading activation** passes activity to connected representations over **weighted connections**.","**Distributed representations** encode an item as a pattern across many units.","**Associative learning**, such as **Hebbian learning**, strengthens links between units that are active together.","**Bottom-up processing** runs from sensory features toward words. **Top-down processing** runs from word knowledge back to the features."]},{"text":"So these ideas connect how knowledge is organized with the time course of recognizing and producing language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p015":[{"text":"**Hubel and Wiesel (1968)** related neural activity to sensory input. Their work came at the same time as the **Cognitive Revolution**.","children":[]},{"text":"They recorded from **simple cells** in **primary visual cortex (V1)**.","children":["A simple cell responds to lines of particular orientations.","It responds within a particular **receptive field**, which is the part of the sensory input the cell responds to."]},{"text":"They changed the orientation and movement of a line and compared the cell's activity.","children":["For upward movement of a line at about **315 degrees**, the cell fired **most strongly**.","For other orientations and movements, the cell fired much less."]},{"text":"The **electrochemical activity** of a cell tells us about its representational and computational properties.","children":["This cell responds selectively. So it can be described as **representing** that combination of features."]},{"text":"So a neuron's activation can stand for a specific combination of stimulus features.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p016":[{"text":"Cells further up the **visual processing stream** respond to more complex patterns.","children":[]},{"text":"In data adapted from **Gross and colleagues (1985)**, one cell in **inferotemporal cortex** responds to the profile of another monkey's face.","children":["For near-profile views, its firing rises strongly above baseline.","For frontal and rear views, its response is much weaker.","Its responses are highest around 80 to 100 degrees of face angle."]},{"text":"It seems natural to say this cell **represents** something complex, such as a face profile, rather than line segments.","children":["Its activation is **graded**. The cell responds a little to one view and much more to another."]},{"text":"In psycholinguistics, **activation** extends this idea metaphorically to **perceptual and conceptual representations**. So a word or a concept can also be more or less active.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p017":[{"text":"Imagine an incredibly simple set of **lexical representations**. These are the stored representations of words.","children":[]},{"text":"Each word is a **node** in a network.","children":["A node is a unit with an activation value.","A specific word can activate its node.","This network has three nodes, for lion, tiger and stripes."]},{"text":"The printed input and the nodes are different levels.","children":["The visible word LION supplies perceptual evidence.","The lion node represents the word the system could recognize from that evidence."]},{"text":"So a node lets a model state how much activity a word receives and how that activity affects recognition.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p018":[{"text":"A word node must respond to many different-looking inputs.","children":[]},{"text":"The word can be printed as LION, lion or Lion.","children":["It can also change in typeface and size.","Each change alters the physical display.","All of these versions should activate the same lion node."]},{"text":"This is the **lack of invariance problem**. The same word has many different sensory forms.","children":["The same problem came up with **phonemes**.","Eventually the **receptive fields** of word nodes would have to be very complicated to handle this."]},{"text":"For now, imagine there are nodes that can be activated by words. So the lion node maps many printed forms onto one word identity.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p019":[{"text":"**Activation can be partial.** A node does not have to be fully on or fully off.","children":[]},{"text":"Here the word LION appears inside a **noise mask**, which is visual noise around the word.","children":["LION contrasts poorly with the mask.","So the input gives only weak evidence for the word.","So the lion node receives only **weak activation**."]},{"text":"The node can be partly active while recognition is still uncertain.","children":["Its activation can rise as more evidence comes through the noise."]},{"text":"So a **graded** activation value captures the stages before a word is identified.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p020":[{"text":"As the stimulus becomes clearer, **activation increases**.","children":[]},{"text":"Two things change together.","children":["In the input, LION becomes easier to tell apart from the noise.","In the model, the lion node becomes **more active**."]},{"text":"Stronger sensory evidence supports the same word more strongly.","children":["So recognition can be modeled as a response that grows with the strength of the input."]},{"text":"That is why changing the display can change how a word is processed.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p021":[{"text":"At some point the **signal-to-noise ratio (SNR)** becomes very favorable. SNR is the strength of the word signal relative to the noise around it.","children":[]},{"text":"At that point, activity for lion is strong enough to count as recognition.","children":["The simple network has **identified** the word lion.","Tiger and stripes stay as other nodes, not the identified word."]},{"text":"This is how the **perceptual identification task** works.","children":["Participants push a button as soon as they can identify the item.","The measure is how long it takes to identify it.","A condition that makes identification easier moves the response earlier."]},{"text":"So the button press links an observable response to the rise of activation in the model.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p022":[{"text":"Two kinds of factors can change how quickly a word is identified in this task.","children":[]},{"text":"**Features of the stimulus itself** concern the target word.","children":["How often the word is encountered is one such feature.","How similar it is to other words is another."]},{"text":"**Features of the experimental environment** concern what surrounds the target.","children":["Having just seen the same word is one such feature.","Having just seen a similar or related word is another."]},{"text":"So the same target can be tested in different contexts. Different targets can also be compared under the same task conditions.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p023":[{"text":"Four factors can change how quickly a word is identified.","children":[]},{"text":"Two factors are **features of the stimulus**.","children":["**Word frequency** is how often a word is encountered. A frequent word can be easier to activate.","Similarity to other words creates **neighborhood** relationships."]},{"text":"Two factors are **features of the experimental environment**.","children":["**Repetition priming** comes from having just seen that same word.","Other priming comes from having just seen a similar or related word."]},{"text":"Frequency and repetition priming are different.","children":["Frequency reflects experience with a word built up over time.","Repetition priming reflects a very recent presentation.","A related prime changes the context without repeating the target."]},{"text":"So explaining an identification advantage means asking what differed between conditions.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p024":[{"text":"Most models of this kind assume that activation **unfolds over time**. This change over time is called **activation dynamics**.","children":[]},{"text":"Activation dynamics can explain **frequency effects**.","children":["The dynamics are **tuned up by experience**.","Words encountered more often become more sensitive.","So frequent words are **easier to activate**."]},{"text":"The **Interactive Activation model** of **Rumelhart and McClelland (1986)** simulates this kind of process.","children":["For the word CAVE, activation rises above competing words while the input supports it.","Then the activation gradually falls."]},{"text":"Different computational models simulate frequency effects in different ways. So frequency is tied to processing through experience.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p025":[{"text":"Activation dynamics also explain **repetition priming**. Repetition priming is faster identification of a word seen again very shortly after the first time.","children":[]},{"text":"Activation does not rise and fall instantly.","children":["Activation may **fall off more slowly than it rises**.","After the word is gone, some activation remains."]},{"text":"Now the word appears again while activation is still high.","children":["The **baseline** for its activation is already higher than usual.","So the node can be activated more easily."]},{"text":"Repetition priming is a basic effect in psychology. That is why seeing a word again soon makes it faster to identify.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p026":[{"text":"Two factors that influence a word's activity have been left out so far.","children":[]},{"text":"The first is **neighborhood effects**, which come from how similar a word is to other words.","children":["The second is priming from a similar or related word.","This priming can be **associative**, **semantic** or **phonological**."]},{"text":"Both factors need something new in the network.","children":["Isolated word nodes can explain changes in one word's own activity.","Repetition involves the same word twice.","These effects involve one word changing a **different** word."]},{"text":"So the network needs links between word representations.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p027":[{"text":"Many language phenomena require that nodes in a network are **connected**. After all, it is a network.","children":[]},{"text":"In a network with lion, tiger and stripes, the nodes are linked.","children":["Lion is connected to tiger.","Tiger is connected to stripes."]},{"text":"The printed input activates lion directly.","children":["A connection can then carry activity from lion to tiger.","This activity is **indirect**, because it arrives through the network rather than from the stimulus."]},{"text":"So a connection changes how one node responds when another is active.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p028":[{"text":"**Spreading activation** explains how one word can prepare the system for a related word.","children":[]},{"text":"The association norms can be built into a network.","children":["The **connection strength** between two nodes is set by their association.","When lion is activated, some activation travels along a **weighted connection** to tiger.","Different connections can pass different amounts of activation."]},{"text":"This is a spreading activation account of **semantic priming**.","children":["Tiger is already partly active from the prime lion.","So tiger is easier to recognize when it appears."]},{"text":"A semantic network can link many concepts. Vehicle links to car and bus. Fire engine links to the vehicles and to fire concepts. So one activated concept can spread to several related ones.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p029":[],"m1-lecture04-p030":[{"text":"STRIPES appears inside a strong **noise mask** at the start of a **perceptual identification** sequence.","children":[]},{"text":"The word has very low contrast.","children":["So the letters are hard to separate from the background.","The word is barely visible in the center of the noise."]},{"text":"The target can start receiving sensory support at this point.","children":["But the evidence is too weak for a confident identification."]},{"text":"So identification cannot happen yet.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p031":[{"text":"The contrast of STRIPES increases. More of its letter pattern stands out against the noise.","children":[]},{"text":"The letters are faint but easier to pick out from the mask.","children":["The input now gives **stronger support** for the same word."]},{"text":"The identity of the target does not change.","children":["Only the quality of the evidence changes."]},{"text":"So clarity changes the evidence while the word stays the same.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p032":[{"text":"STRIPES becomes more **identifiable** as its contrast improves.","children":[]},{"text":"The letters now stand apart from the surrounding noise.","children":["The evidence for the word keeps growing."]},{"text":"In the perceptual identification task, the participant responds when the evidence is enough to recognize the word.","children":["The point of response depends on the quality of the stimulus."]},{"text":"So better stimulus quality brings the **identification response** closer.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p033":[{"text":"The clearer STRIPES gives stronger word information than the heavily masked versions.","children":[]},{"text":"The noise field is the same kind as before.","children":["Only the letter pattern has become clearer."]},{"text":"A participant who recognizes the word can now make the **identification response**.","children":["The response reflects **recognizing the target word**.","It is not a judgment about whether stripes go with a particular animal."]},{"text":"So the task measures recognition, not association.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p034":[{"text":"STRIPES is now shown at high contrast. The dark letters stand out from the gray noise.","children":[]},{"text":"The target is easy to tell apart from the mask.","children":["Recognizing it activates the **stripes node** in the network."]},{"text":"This identified word can act as a **prime**. A prime is an earlier item that may change how a later item is processed.","children":["Its effect on another word depends on the **connections** between their nodes."]},{"text":"So identifying STRIPES prepares a test of what it does to a later word.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p035":[{"text":"LION is the **target** in a second perceptual identification sequence.","children":[]},{"text":"It starts with weak contrast against the noise mask.","children":["So its letter pattern gives little usable information.","The short word sits in the center of the noise and is hard to read."]},{"text":"The target can be separated from its recent context.","children":["A **priming** account asks whether earlier experience changes how quickly LION is recognized."]},{"text":"So the sequence tests whether the preceding word speeds up recognizing LION.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p036":[{"text":"The contrast of LION increases.","children":[]},{"text":"LION is now faintly visible in the center of the mask.","children":["The letter pattern starts to separate from the background noise.","So the **perceptual evidence** for the word gets stronger."]},{"text":"The same target can produce a **changing internal response**.","children":["This happens because the quality of its sensory evidence changes."]},{"text":"So recognition becomes more likely as the display gets clearer.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p037":[{"text":"LION becomes clearer inside the mask. The four letters have more contrast than before.","children":[]},{"text":"A perceptual identification response can happen when the evidence supports recognizing the word.","children":["The **time** of that response is what gets measured."]},{"text":"A priming comparison asks whether recognition happens **sooner** after one preceding context than after another.","children":["LION is the common target in that comparison."]},{"text":"So response time for the same target is how priming is detected.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p038":[{"text":"LION is now legible, while the noise mask is still present.","children":[]},{"text":"The stimulus gives substantial evidence for the word lion.","children":["In the network, that evidence raises the **activation of the lion node**."]},{"text":"Two sources can add to the lion node's state.","children":["One is the **clarity** of the target itself.","The other is any **activation left by the context**."]},{"text":"So recognition happens from a state built by both the input and the context.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p039":[{"text":"The clear LION display completes the identification sequence. The strong word signal is easy to tell apart from the noise.","children":[]},{"text":"The lion node can receive activation in two ways.","children":["It can be activated **directly** by the printed word.","It can also be influenced **through connections** with other nodes."]},{"text":"The difference between direct input and activity arriving through the network is central to this experiment. So the question is whether STRIPES helped LION through the network.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p040":[{"text":"Activity flows throughout the network. So activating stripes can **prime lion** even though the two words are **not directly associated**.","children":[]},{"text":"The path runs through a middle word.","children":["Stripes is connected to tiger.","Tiger is connected to lion.","So activation from stripes reaches lion by way of tiger."]},{"text":"This is called **mediated priming**. The priming passes through a middle word, here tiger.","children":["A model with only direct associations has no route from stripes to lion.","So it could not explain the effect."]},{"text":"That is why this effect gives **compelling evidence for spreading activation**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p041":[{"text":"The stripes and lion experiment is very representative of psycholinguistics.","children":[]},{"text":"It has **careful study design**.","children":["It finds **small effects**."]},{"text":"The result is **counterintuitive**. Why would stripes prime lion?","children":["Stripes and lion are not obvious direct associates.","Stripes primes lion because activity travels through the connections each word has with tiger.","A model with only direct associations cannot explain this effect.","So even a small effect can tell the two accounts apart."]},{"text":"Three broader issues remain. These are top-down and bottom-up processing, learning and distributed representations.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p042":[{"text":"**Bottom-up processing** carries information from visual features up to letters and then to words. **Top-down processing** lets an active word send activation back to the letters that fit it.","children":[]},{"text":"The **Interactive Activation network** after **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)** has three levels.","children":["Features are at the bottom.","Letters are in the middle.","Words such as WORK, WORD, WEAR and WEAK are at the top."]},{"text":"In the example, blots cover part of the last letter of WORK.","children":["The visible features fit more than one letter.","WORK becomes active at the word level and sends activation back down to K.","So the output for **K rises strongly** over time, while R and D stay low."]},{"text":"Top-down processing is one way of modeling **pattern completion**, which Menn discusses at length. So word knowledge helps complete a letter that is only partly visible.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p043":[{"text":"**Learning** explains how all of this knowledge gets into the brain. The core idea is extremely simple but powerful.","children":[]},{"text":"**Hebbian learning** is summed up as \"neurons that fire together, wire together.\"","children":["When two units are active together, the connection between them gets stronger.","The real process is more complicated than this.","But the neural learning mechanisms that are relatively well understood are **associative**."]},{"text":"In the bell and food example, a bell signal and a food signal both feed a salivation unit.","children":["The bell input and the food input are active together.","So the **synapse** from the bell to the salivation unit is strengthened.","An initially weak influence becomes more effective."]},{"text":"Historically, these mechanisms were considered **insufficient** to learn anything important about language.","children":["More recent arguments ask what role they play and how **innate knowledge** might constrain them.","This matters because **large language models (LLMs)** learn many structural features of language with little more than this."]},{"text":"So associative learning changes the network that will process future input.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p044":[{"text":"**Distributed representations** let associative learning pick up fairly sophisticated patterns. A distributed representation encodes an item as a pattern across many units.","children":[]},{"text":"**Generativity** is very important for language.","children":["To generalize, an associative model must be able to represent **similarity**.","Similar items have overlapping patterns.","So what is learned about one item can carry over to similar items."]},{"text":"Robins and canaries are the case here.","children":["What you know about robins and canaries can **generalize** to new birds as you learn about them."]},{"text":"The network takes an **item** input and a **relation** input.","children":["For canary with the relation can, the active outputs include grow, move, fly and sing.","Hidden layers link the item and relation to that set of properties."]},{"text":"So shared internal patterns let a model respond to combinations it has not memorized one by one.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p045":[{"text":"Five **neurally inspired metaphors** give a vocabulary for psycholinguistics.","children":[]},{"text":"**Activation** is how active a representation is, and it changes as input is processed.","children":["**Spreading activation** lets one representation influence another through connections.","**Top-down and bottom-up processing** describe the direction of influence between levels.","**Associative learning**, such as Hebbian learning, changes connection strengths.","**Distributed representations** give a basis for similarity and generalization."]},{"text":"**Priming experiments** help reveal what people's **lexical networks** might look like.","children":["They measure how processing one item changes the processing of another."]},{"text":"So these ideas connect representations, mechanisms and observable behavior.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p046":[],"m1-lecture04-p047":[],"m1-lecture05-p001":[],"m1-lecture05-p002":[{"text":"**Phrenology** tried to relate brain structure to function by measuring the shape of the skull. A simplified phrenological chart assigns head regions to language, memory, thinking, love and other categories.","children":[]},{"text":"Its reasoning rested on three premises.","children":["The first premise says that **different parts of the brain** are responsible for different processes.","The second premise says that **aptitude** is related to differences in brain region size.","The third premise says that **brain region size influences skull shape**."]},{"text":"From these premises, careful skull measurements were supposed to reveal relationships between skull shape and brain functions. No one believes in phrenology anymore.","children":[]},{"text":"The premises are not equally wrong.","children":["The first premise stays a serious idea, because brain parts can contribute differently to behavior.","The second premise needs a dependable link from an ability to the size of a region.","The third premise fails, because skull shape is not a sensitive measure of brain shape."]},{"text":"So the idea of specialized brain parts survives, but reading the skull to measure them does not.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p003":[{"text":"**Phrenology** had two important problems, one in its anatomy and one in its psychology.","children":[]},{"text":"The first problem was **inappropriate anatomical measurement**.","children":["Skull shape is not a sensitive measure of brain shape.","So measuring the head cannot identify a brain mechanism."]},{"text":"The second problem was an **unconstrained definition of functions**.","children":["Broad social labels were treated as if each one named a single mental function.","Studies of prisoners aimed to identify regions associated with \"criminality\".","Studies of women with different numbers of children aimed to find the locus of \"philopropogenitiveness\"."]},{"text":"A convincing structure and function study needs a valid anatomical measure. It also needs a clear account of the process being measured.","children":[]},{"text":"So phrenology failed on both sides of the relationship it tried to find.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p004":[{"text":"**Flourens** was an early and vociferous critic of phrenology. He studied the brain with **experimental lesions**, which means damaging or removing brain tissue and then observing behavior.","children":[]},{"text":"He worked mainly with pigeons and rabbits.","children":["His experiments established **distinct functions** for the cerebral cortex, the cerebellum and mid-brain structures.","So large parts of the brain did differ in what they do."]},{"text":"Within the cortex, he **failed to find any correspondence** between specific regions and specific senses.","children":["He insisted instead that the **size of the lesion** was critical.","In his view, when one sense was lost, all were lost together.","For example, removing most of the cortex makes sight, hearing and other senses disappear together."]},{"text":"So Flourens argued that loss depended on how much tissue was destroyed. He did not accept a precise map of sensory centers.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p005":[{"text":"**Pierre Paul Broca** was a neurologist who studied **aphasia**, a loss of language ability after brain damage.","children":[]},{"text":"In **1861**, Broca presented the case of a patient called **\"Tan\"**.","children":["Tan had lost the ability to speak after a brain injury decades earlier.","Broca gave the presentation to the newly established Society of Anthropology.","He was also the founding president of that society."]},{"text":"Tan's abilities were split.","children":["For **speaking**, Tan could produce only **one jargon syllable**.","For **everything else**, he seemed to be \"in possession of his senses\".","For **understanding**, he seemed to follow what was being said."]},{"text":"This was not the first observation of a specific loss of speech.","children":[]},{"text":"So Tan's case suggested that producing speech and understanding speech can be damaged separately.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p006":[{"text":"**Tan's brain** is on display at the Musee Dupuytren. Its anatomical description begins with a **large lesion**.","children":[]},{"text":"The lesion definitely included the **third convolution of the frontal lobe**. This region is also called the **inferior frontal gyrus**.","children":[]},{"text":"From this case comes **Broca's area**.","children":["Broca's area is a portion of the **left frontal lobe** that is important for speech."]},{"text":"**Hughlings Jackson (1874)** raised a contemporary criticism.","children":["He wrote that \"to locate the damage which destroys speech and to locate speech are two different things\".","Damage in one place can stop speech.","That does not mean speech itself lives in that place."]},{"text":"So a lesion links a region to a lost ability, but it does not show everything that region does.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p007":[{"text":"In the **1870s**, German neurologists such as **Lichtheim** and **Wernicke** made more systematic observations of aphasia.","children":[]},{"text":"They explained specific losses as **\"disconnections\" between \"centers\"**.","children":["A center is a proposed brain location for one language function.","A disconnection is damage to the pathway joining two centers."]},{"text":"Each loss came from a different broken pathway.","children":["For **understanding spoken words**, the loss might come from a disconnection between \"A\" and \"B\".","For **verbatim repetition**, the loss might come from a disconnection between \"A\" and \"M\".","In Lichtheim's house model, A stands for sound images, B for concepts and M for movement images."]},{"text":"This approach of describing centers for specific functions is an ancestor of **modularity**.","children":[]},{"text":"So the pathway between centers became part of the explanation, not only the centers themselves.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p008":[{"text":"**Sigmund Freud** critiqued the **\"diagram-drawing\" school** of aphasia research in his early neurological work.","children":[]},{"text":"He felt it was naive to expect the brain to follow psychological categories.","children":["Categories like \"word forms\" and \"concepts\" describe behavior.","Freud doubted that the brain is organized around those same categories."]},{"text":"He proposed instead that language emerges from **\"fields\"**.","children":["These fields connect perceptual and motor abilities to **\"drives\"**.","This is a **holist critique**, because it treats language as spread across connected systems."]},{"text":"His critique raises a **durable tension** in cognitive neuroscience.","children":["One question asks whether brain area functions relate directly to cognitive functions.","Another asks whether the brain is really organized according to our descriptions of cognition."]},{"text":"So a useful category for behavior is not automatically a separate part of the brain.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p009":[{"text":"**Dronkers and colleagues (2007)** used **MRI** to reexamine Leborgne's brain, the patient known as Tan. MRI let them assess the extent and location of damage very precisely.","children":[]},{"text":"The scans revealed damage that surface inspection had missed.","children":["The lesion is **very deep** and includes the **insula**.","There is also heavy **white matter damage** to the **superior longitudinal fasciculus**.","This fiber tract connects the front and back of the cerebral cortex."]},{"text":"Other work by this group linked a speech disorder to a different region.","children":["The **insula**, not Broca's area, is strongly associated with **speech apraxia**.","Speech apraxia is the condition Broca described in his patients."]},{"text":"So the lost speech in Broca's cases may come from deeper damage, not just from Broca's area.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p010":[{"text":"Broca is still discussed 150 years later, even though his work was crude.","children":[]},{"text":"His **characterization of behavior** was crude.","children":["Linguistics then focused on historical issues, such as how languages were related.","Linguists also worked on reconstructing ancient languages.","Experimental psychology had not really been invented yet.","So observations of behavior were necessarily informal."]},{"text":"His **anatomical localization** was also crude, and probably wrong.","children":["Fine-grained descriptions of the cerebral cortex were still in their infancy.","**Brodmann's map** of cortical regions was not published until **1909**."]},{"text":"So Broca's limits came from the methods of his time.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p011":[{"text":"**Broca's presentation of Tan** is seen as a **watershed moment** in neuropsychology.","children":[]},{"text":"Other researchers showed similar things around the same time.","children":["**The Daxes** reported related observations.","**Wernicke** also reported a case.","Wernicke's patient was **fluent** but produced **meaningless gibberish**.","If anything, Wernicke's data are more interesting because of that contrast with Tan."]},{"text":"For a variety of reasons, Broca's case still became the turning point.","children":["It turned relating **specific functions to specific brain regions** into a **respectable pursuit**."]},{"text":"So Broca's lasting importance is that he made localization a serious research question.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p012":[],"m1-lecture05-p013":[{"text":"**Cognitive neuropsychology** studies how brain damage affects mental functions. It depends on **cognitive psychology** for its definitions of those functions.","children":[]},{"text":"Much of what was learned about structure and function in the 20th century came from brain-damaged individuals. These were experimental observations of their behavior.","children":[]},{"text":"**Cognitive theory** provides hypotheses about which operations might be separable.","children":["One question asks if recognizing the **abstract forms of words** can be separated from computing their **meaning**.","If damage harms one ability but spares the other, the two are **dissociated**.","Tasks can separate these demands.","**Physical identity** matching compares letters by appearance.","**Name identity** matching compares letters by name.","**Rule identity** matching sorts letters as vowels or consonants."]},{"text":"**Quantitative measurements** give a way to test such questions.","children":["They allow **statistical comparison** of how well each ability survives.","This is critical because **deficits are rarely complete**."]},{"text":"So a clear theory and careful measurement together make a dissociation meaningful.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p014":[{"text":"**Anatomical imaging** locates brain injuries at high resolution in living patients.","children":[]},{"text":"Before imaging, scientists had to wait for patients to \"come to autopsy\".","children":["Only then could they find the site of the lesion.","Imaging removed that wait."]},{"text":"A scan of **H.M.'s** middle temporal lobes is one example.","children":["His damage was the result of surgery."]},{"text":"The limit from **Hughlings Jackson (1874)** still applies.","children":["Locating the damage that destroys speech differs from locating speech.","Studies of brain-injured patients show what behavior looks like after a region is damaged.","They tell only **indirectly** what the region does when intact."]},{"text":"So imaging sharpens the anatomy, but the explanation still needs a theory.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p015":[{"text":"**Metabolic neuroimaging** lets researchers study brains in action. **Posner and colleagues (1988)** described **PET** studies that used radio-labeled oxygen as a marker for activity.","children":[]},{"text":"Metabolic methods like PET and **fMRI** have three key limits.","children":["The methods are **metabolic**, so they measure an index of **oxygen consumption**, not neural activity itself.","The methods are **slow**, because metabolic processes unfold over **seconds**. Important neural events can occur in **milliseconds** or less.","They have only **fair spatial resolution**. FMRI is better than PET. Resolution is still on the order of **millimeters**. Important events happen at the scale of **microns**."]},{"text":"So these images estimate activity indirectly, slowly and coarsely.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p016":[{"text":"**Subtraction logic** applies cognitive psychology to metabolic imaging data.","children":[]},{"text":"A claim that \"x part of the brain is active during y task\" does not work.","children":["There is activity **all over the brain all of the time**.","So active regions alone cannot identify a task."]},{"text":"Every study needs a **control condition**.","children":["The control shares most demands with the task condition.","Subtracting the control image from the task image leaves the **difference in activity**.","A motor image minus a rest image leaves a much smaller region."]},{"text":"**Posner and colleagues (1988)** used this logic to separate word form identification from meaning.","children":["Their language tasks shared visual input and responses.","The tasks differed in whether **word meaning** had to be accessed.","Subtracting the simpler task from the meaning task isolates **semantic processing**."]},{"text":"So the leftover activity belongs to the one operation the conditions differ in.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p017":[{"text":"**Posner and colleagues (1988)** found one region active across several **semantic** contrasts.","children":[]},{"text":"Each contrast compared a meaning task with a simpler task.","children":["For **visual monitoring**, people watched words for dangerous animals, compared with passively viewing words.","For **visual generating**, people produced uses for written words, compared with repeating them.","For **auditory generating**, people produced uses for heard words, compared with repeating them."]},{"text":"All three contrasts activated the **left inferior frontal** region.","children":["The shared activity appears for both seen and heard words.","So it links this region to **semantic processing** of words."]},{"text":"Interestingly, this region corresponds **roughly to Broca's area**.","children":[]},{"text":"So the region tied to lost speech in Broca's cases appears again in modern semantic tasks.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p018":[{"text":"**A modern localization claim** must be compared with phrenology on how its evidence was obtained.","children":[]},{"text":"The question \"Is this better than phrenology?\" is rhetorical but worth exploring.","children":[]},{"text":"Phrenology failed for two reasons.","children":["It used an **invalid measure**, skull shape.","It used **undefined functions**, such as \"criminality\"."]},{"text":"Imaging studies answer both problems in part.","children":["They measure an **index of brain activity** instead of skull shape.","They define the operation through a **controlled task contrast**."]},{"text":"An attractive image of an active region still settles nothing by itself.","children":["The contrast must truly isolate the proposed operation.","The measurement must support linking that operation to the region."]},{"text":"So imaging is better than phrenology when its definitions and measurements are sound.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p019":[{"text":"**Posner and colleagues (1988)** argued for localizing **component operations**, not whole tasks.","children":[]},{"text":"These operations act on **visual**, **phonological** and **semantic codes**.","children":[]},{"text":"Whole tasks involve many coordinated computations.","children":["Reading a word needs several of them.","Forming a visual image needs several of them.","Shifting attention needs several of them.","So a complete task is not assigned to one brain area."]},{"text":"The PET evidence **complemented lesion evidence**.","children":["It related cognitive distinctions to activity during performance.","Averaged blood-flow patterns suggested **consistency** across participants.","The participants were right-handed and had good reading skills."]},{"text":"So their answer to \"Is this better than phrenology?\" is to localize defined operations, not broad traits or whole tasks.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p020":[{"text":"**Language** has turned up at key moments in debates about **localization of function**.","children":[]},{"text":"It appeared in two major moments.","children":["**Broca's presentation of Tan** helped establish **lesion-behavior analysis** in neuropsychology.","Some of the first **PET** and **fMRI** studies used activation of **Broca's area** in language tasks.","Those studies took it as evidence that cognitive functions could be localized."]},{"text":"Language suits these investigations because it splits into **separable abilities**.","children":["Speaking can be lost while understanding seems spared, as in Tan.","Understanding words, repeating words and judging meaning place different demands on processing.","These differences give researchers clear contrasts for lesion studies and control conditions."]},{"text":"So language offers distinct, testable parts for relating function to structure.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p021":[],"m1-lecture06-p001":[],"m1-lecture06-p002":[{"text":"A **formal symbolic system** describes language with abstract symbols and rules. This way of describing language is very old. It uses terms similar to those used in **Artificial Intelligence**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Experimental psychology** in the 1950s and 1960s was actively hostile to talk of an **internal state**.","children":["Behaviorism explained learning through observable stimuli, responses and consequences.","An internal state cannot be observed directly, so psychologists of that era avoided it."]},{"text":"Two works framed the debate.","children":["**Skinner's Verbal Behavior** tried to describe how language could be learned by **operant conditioning**.","**Chomsky's (1959) review** of Verbal Behavior challenged that account. It is better remembered than the book itself."]},{"text":"Chomsky treated a **grammar** as an internal system. A grammar can characterize sentences a person has never encountered.","children":[]},{"text":"So Chomsky's review was an important moment in **the Cognitive Revolution**. That revolution brought internal mental representations back into explanations of language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p003":[{"text":"The formal tradition comes from interest in **Artificial Intelligence**. That interest dates back to the beginning of the **digital computer**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Alan Turing** thought about this question a lot.","children":["He developed decryption devices for the British Army during World War II."]},{"text":"Early computers were built as **universal function approximators**.","children":["A universal function approximator is a machine that can carry out many kinds of calculations and logical operations.","No one had built a machine that did so many different kinds of operations before.","Earlier machines did a single fixed task."]},{"text":"This flexibility raised a new question.","children":["Researchers asked how the computer differed from **human intelligence**, if at all.","The difference could lie in the procedures, the representations, or something else."]},{"text":"So language research became tied to Artificial Intelligence. If language is symbols and procedures, a computer can test what those procedures accomplish.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p004":[{"text":"The **Turing test** judges a machine by how it behaves in conversation. It grows out of a party game called the **imitation game**.","children":[]},{"text":"The imitation game has three players.","children":["**A and B** are a man and a woman.","**C** is an examiner. C's job is to figure out the gender of A and B.","**A** tries to fool C.","**B** tries to help C guess the correct answer."]},{"text":"The test replaces either A or B with a **computer**.","children":["If C does not notice the swap, the computer has demonstrated **intelligence**.","The examiner never inspects the program. The examiner only judges the conversation."]},{"text":"So the Turing test turns the broad question of intelligence into observable conversational performance. **Natural language** is the medium the examiner uses to decide.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p005":[{"text":"The Turing test has several interesting features. Each one points toward ordinary language use and learning.","children":[]},{"text":"The first feature is the role of **natural language**.","children":["The machine must interpret questions and produce fitting answers."]},{"text":"The second feature is **deception**.","children":["One player tries to fool the examiner.","So a respondent has to handle an examiner who may be trying to expose it."]},{"text":"The third feature is what Turing left out.","children":["Turing explicitly excluded **expert behaviors**.","Calculating large sums is one excluded behavior.","Composing poetry is another.","Broad conversation is a different task from one expert skill."]},{"text":"The fourth feature is Turing's conclusion about **learning**.","children":["The best way to get an intelligent machine is to build something with the cognitive capabilities of a **child**.","That machine then learns to be more like an **adult**."]},{"text":"So Turing treated intelligence as something that develops through experience. It is not supplied as a finished list of adult answers.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p006":[{"text":"**Newell and Simon's General Problem Solver** is a later example of **GOFAI**. GOFAI is the approach that models intelligence as operations on explicit symbols.","children":[]},{"text":"The program receives an abstract description of a formal problem.","children":["It then applies procedures to symbols to find a solution.","Each configuration of the problem is a **state**. A legal move changes one state into another state.","A solution is a sequence of legal moves that reaches the **goal state**."]},{"text":"The program was very good at games and puzzles.","children":["The **Tower of Hanoi** is one example. Each arrangement of disks is a state, and each legal move links two states.","**Chess** is another example."]},{"text":"It was also good at mathematics and logic.","children":["It handled **geometry proofs**.","It handled **algebra word problems**."]},{"text":"So the General Problem Solver treats solving a problem as searching for a path through states. That search works only once the states, goal and legal moves are spelled out.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p007":[{"text":"Early AI emphasized **formal problems** for two reasons. One reason was technology, and the other was theory.","children":[]},{"text":"The first reason was **technology**.","children":["Inputs to computers were still very limited. They came through punchcards and machine language.","There was no way to input a high-resolution image and ask the computer to find the face.","So explicit symbols were far easier to give a computer than rich perceptual information."]},{"text":"The second reason was **theoretical commitments**.","children":["Researchers believed **rational behavior** of this kind is what intelligence looks like.","You can train a **pigeon** to guide a missile, but the pigeon does not know what it is doing.","There is a deep argument about whether AI programs know what they are doing either.","AI programs at least **represent goal states**. They also choose procedures for reaching them."]},{"text":"So formal puzzles fit both the machines and the theory of the time. That is why early programs modeled this kind of intelligence.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p008":[{"text":"**Chomsky's critique** argued that knowing a language means having an internal **grammar**. A grammar is a system that enumerates sentences and gives each one a meaningful **structural description**.","children":[]},{"text":"A grammar captures several remarkable abilities.","children":["A speaker can tell **sentences from nonsentences**.","A speaker can understand **new sentences**, in part.","A speaker can notice **ambiguities**."]},{"text":"New sentences are not linked to familiar ones by any simple **similarity**.","children":["The similarity is not formal, semantic or statistical.","It is not identity of grammatical frame either.","So Chomsky called talk of **generalization** here pointless and empty."]},{"text":"A new item counts as a sentence because the internalized grammar **generates** it.","children":["We understand a new sentence partly because we can work out how the grammar **derives** it."]},{"text":"All normal children acquire comparable grammars of great complexity with remarkable speed.","children":["Chomsky took this as evidence that humans are **specially designed** to do it.","He proposed a data-handling or **hypothesis-formulating** ability of unknown character."]},{"text":"So Chomsky said language needs an internal grammar, not conditioning.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p009":[{"text":"Chomsky's first point is that **linguistic knowledge** is a **grammar**. A grammar is a set of abstract rules that produce grammatical sentences.","children":[]},{"text":"The number of grammatical sentences is **infinite**.","children":["A speaker has heard only a small part of them.","Yet a speaker can make a **grammaticality judgment** for any new sentence.","A grammaticality judgment is a decision about whether a sentence is well formed in the language.","That ability is remarkable because the sentence was never heard before."]},{"text":"The grammar also explains ambiguity.","children":["One string of words can receive more than one structure.","So knowing the words alone does not fix the meaning."]},{"text":"These judgments become central evidence.","children":["People's **intuitions** about whether sentences are grammatical are the key data for the study of language.","The researcher asks which structures a person accepts, not only which sentences the person has said."]},{"text":"So the target of study is the rule system that makes all possible sentences available.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p010":[{"text":"Chomsky's second point attacks **stimulus generalization**. Stimulus generalization is a behaviorist idea. A response trained to one stimulus spreads to similar stimuli.","children":[]},{"text":"Generalization needs similarity between the trained item and the new item.","children":["Grammatical sentences have no obvious similarities to one another.","Their sounds can differ. Their meanings can differ. Their frames can differ.","So similarity cannot link a new sentence to familiar ones."]},{"text":"A grammar can generate an **infinite number of sentences**.","children":["Stimulus generalization cannot cover that infinite set."]},{"text":"Chomsky offered a different explanation.","children":["We recognize a new sentence because the **internalized grammar** generates it.","It does not need to match one remembered sentence."]},{"text":"So a theory of language has to explain how people recognize abstract grammatical relations. Spreading a trained response to a similar stimulus is a different process.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p011":[{"text":"**Stimulus generalization** in behaviorism depends on concrete similarity between stimuli.","children":[]},{"text":"A **pigeon** can be trained to peck for a **green light**.","children":["Its pecking then generalizes to similar colors.","Responding is strongest near green. It drops as the test color becomes less similar.","This pattern is a **generalization gradient**."]},{"text":"Another pigeon does a **magnitude discrimination** task.","children":["It responds according to amounts, so number is another dimension of comparison."]},{"text":"The cases where operant conditioning generalizes share a feature.","children":["They depend on relatively concrete similarity, such as **color** or **number**.","Each dimension can be varied step by step."]},{"text":"It is not clear how this extends to **abstract concepts**.","children":["Grammar is an abstract relation.","No concrete dimension like color links one grammatical sentence to another."]},{"text":"So a conditioning account of language must say what plays the role of color for grammar. That is why generalization gradients do not explain sentences.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p012":[{"text":"**Stimulus control** is Skinner's idea that some aspect of the environment controls a response.","children":[]},{"text":"Skinner's typical example is music.","children":["A person hears a piece of **music** and responds with an utterance.","The music is treated as the stimulus that controls the utterance."]},{"text":"Chomsky said other examples only add to the **general mystification**.","children":["A **proper noun** is one such example. Skinner treated it as a response to a stimulus."]},{"text":"The trouble is identifying the stimulus.","children":["A piece of music has many properties.","A speaker can respond to any of them.","The stimulus has to be identified **independently** of the utterance.","Otherwise the utterance itself decides what counts as its cause."]},{"text":"So stimulus control explains an utterance only if the stimulus can be named before the response.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p013":[{"text":"Chomsky argued that **stimulus control** cannot predict speech. We do not know what the current stimuli are until the speaker responds.","children":[]},{"text":"Chomsky's reading is that Skinner posed the **wrong question**.","children":["Skinner does not only have a bad theory of language.","His question itself cannot be answered."]},{"text":"What people say is decided by many things.","children":["**Social interactions** affect it.","**Cognitive processes** affect it.","**Previous experiences** affect it.","Much of this has nothing to do with language.","So naming the stimulus only after the response makes the prediction **circular**."]},{"text":"Chomsky proposed a **tractable problem** instead.","children":["Researchers study the **structure** of what people say.","From that structure, they try to derive the set of **rules** that produces it."]},{"text":"So a grammar describes which sentences are possible. It does not explain why one person says one thing at one moment.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p014":[{"text":"**Grammatical structure** and meaning can come apart. Chomsky (1955) gave a pair of sentences to make this point.","children":[]},{"text":"Neither sentence, nor any of their parts, had ever occurred in an English speaker's past experience.","children":["Sentence (a) is \"colorless green ideas sleep furiously\".","Sentence (b) is \"furiously sleep ideas green colorless\"."]},{"text":"For **sentence (a)**, the words follow English structure. Adjectives come before the noun, and a verb and adverb follow. So it is **grammatical**.","children":["For **sentence (b)**, the same words are reversed. The structure is gone, so it is **not grammatical**."]},{"text":"So a speaker's judgment cannot come from having heard the sentence before. It comes from the **structure** the grammar assigns.","children":[]},{"text":"A second example points the other way.","children":["The warning \"Car! Getting towed! Yours!\" is **ungrammatical**.","Yet the stimulus that likely controlled it is perfectly clear.","So communicating successfully and being grammatical are separate questions."]}],"m1-lecture06-p015":[{"text":"Chomsky describes language at the **computational level of analysis**. This term comes from **David Marr**, a visual neuroscientist.","children":[]},{"text":"Marr said cognitive systems have three **levels of analysis**.","children":["The **computational level** says what problem the system solves.","The **algorithmic level** says what representations and procedures solve it.","The **implementational level** says how those procedures are physically carried out."]},{"text":"The levels ask different questions about the same ability.","children":["A claim can be informative at one level without answering the others.","Separating them makes clear whether a claim is about the task, the procedure or the physical machinery."]},{"text":"Chomsky's abstract grammar is a **computational** description.","children":["It states what language processing must achieve."]},{"text":"So Marr's levels place Chomsky's theory at the level of the task itself. An example makes the levels concrete.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p016":[{"text":"A **cash register** makes Marr's three levels concrete. This example borrows heavily from the teaching of **Sharon Goldwater**.","children":[]},{"text":"Each level describes the cash register differently.","children":["At the **computational level**, it takes item prices and determines the amount due. Given the payment, it computes the change due.","At the **algorithmic level**, it uses **addition** for the price. It uses **subtraction** for the change.","At the **implementational level**, the machinery does not matter."]},{"text":"A credit card reader or a computer program counts as a cash register too.","children":["A mechanical register uses one kind of machinery.","An electronic register or card reader uses another kind.","Each one performs the same functions."]},{"text":"For the same prices and payment, the change due is fixed by the task.","children":["Changing the machinery does not change that answer."]},{"text":"So one function can have many physical forms. That is why the task can be described without knowing the hardware.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p017":[{"text":"Marr called problems that break down cleanly into his levels **Type I problems**.","children":[]},{"text":"Viewing language as a Type I problem divides the work.","children":["Researchers can work at the **computational level**. For language, this is **Universal Grammar**.","Researchers can also work at the **algorithmic level**. This covers specific processes and how they apply in different languages.","They do not have to worry about the **implementational level**."]},{"text":"The implementational level includes several things.","children":["It includes the **errors** people make.","It includes **memory limitations**.","It includes **associative learning**."]},{"text":"The order of events matters.","children":["**Chomsky's critique** came about a decade before **Marr's** theoretical work.","Both were influential in artificial intelligence and in theoretical work in the **GOFAI** tradition."]},{"text":"So the Type I view lets grammar be studied as a system of its own. Processing details are set aside until the computation is known.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p018":[{"text":"Marr also described **Type 2** problems. These do not break down cleanly into levels.","children":[]},{"text":"Marr first linked his levels to Chomsky.","children":["Chomsky's **competence theory** for English syntax is exactly what Marr meant by a computational theory.","Both care little about the details of the algorithms that run the computation.","Algorithms are still hard to devise. But first one must know what they are supposed to do.","A problem that divides this way has a **Type 1 theory**."]},{"text":"Many problems of biological information processing have a Type 1 theory.","children":["There is no reason they all should."]},{"text":"A **Type 2 theory** arises from many processes acting at once.","children":["The problem is solved by the simultaneous action of a considerable number of processes.","Their interaction is its own **simplest description**."]},{"text":"So a Type 2 problem cannot be split into an abstract computation plus details. The interacting processes themselves are the explanation.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p019":[{"text":"Marr suggested that the **grammar of natural language** may be a **Type 2** problem.","children":[]},{"text":"Marr described the purpose of language this way.","children":["A speaker holds a **data structure** that is not one-dimensional.","Language turns it into a **one-dimensional** form. That form is sent as a **sequential utterance**.","The listener then rebuilds a rough copy of the original in their head."]},{"text":"This raises a possibility about **English syntax**.","children":["There may be no Type 1 theory of the kind **transformational grammar** tries to define.","The constraints of syntax may resemble **wired-in conventions**. Those conventions are useful ways of carrying out this tedious but vital operation.","They may not be deep principles about the nature of intelligence."]},{"text":"An abstract theory of syntax may be an **illusion**.","children":["It may only approximate what the processes really do.","Marr compared it to **Julesz's second order statistics theory**. That theory only approximates the processes that carry out texture vision."]},{"text":"So grammar may have a Type 2 theory rather than a Type 1 theory.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p020":[{"text":"Details of how language is **implemented in the brain** might be unavoidable in a theory of language.","children":[]},{"text":"This follows from the Type 2 idea.","children":["Linguistic behavior may come from interacting processes.","If it does, learning, memory, perception and action belong in the explanation.","So the brain's organization may shape the organization of language itself."]},{"text":"A whole alternative approach to language research is based on this hypothesis about the **brain**.","children":["The first approach starts from an abstract grammar that the brain must carry out.","The alternative starts from the learner and the processing system.","It asks what language-like organization can develop from them."]},{"text":"So the two approaches differ in where an explanation starts. The alternative treats implementation as part of the theory of language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p021":[{"text":"The alternative approach goes by several names. These are **emergentism**, **statistical learning** and **parallel distributed processing**.","children":[]},{"text":"Its strong commitments are at the **implementational level**.","children":[]},{"text":"The first commitment is **distributed representations**.","children":["These representations are non-hierarchical.","A representation is spread over a group of units. It is like activity patterns over groups of neurons."]},{"text":"The second commitment is **gradual learning**.","children":["Learning adjusts the strength of connections. It is like adjusting the strength of synaptic connections.","Experience changes how an input pattern leads to an activity pattern."]},{"text":"The third commitment is a focus on **microstructure**.","children":["Many small interactions together produce a larger regularity.","The division between **levels of analysis** is more graded."]},{"text":"So this approach expects the learning mechanism to shape the apparent rules in behavior.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p022":[{"text":"**Elman (1990)** built **simple recurrent networks**. These networks capture different aspects of language performance in the emergentist framework.","children":[]},{"text":"The model has only one task.","children":["It tries to **predict the next item** it is going to see.","It gradually learns to do this with **experience**."]},{"text":"The network passes information through several groups of units.","children":["The current **input** activates the **hidden units**.","The hidden units produce an **output** prediction.","A copy of the hidden activity goes into **context units**.","When the next input arrives, the context units feed the hidden units too."]},{"text":"Some connections change and one does not.","children":["The connections that learn are adjusted to improve the prediction.","The connection that copies hidden activity into context is fixed."]},{"text":"So the network's prediction depends on both the current item and what came before. Its context gives it a limited memory of the sequence.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p023":[{"text":"A **letter-prediction model** built on these principles can find **words**. It was never given spaces.","children":[]},{"text":"The model receives a continuous string of letters.","children":["The string is \"Manyyearsagoaboyandgirllivedbytheseatheyplayedhappily\".","It comes out organized as \"many years ago a boy and girl lived by the sea they played happily\"."]},{"text":"The model's only job is to predict the next letter.","children":["**Prediction error** measures how wrong each guess is."]},{"text":"The error changes in a regular way.","children":["At the **start of a word**, the error is high. Many letters could come next.","**Within a word**, the error **goes down**. Each letter limits what can follow.","At the next word boundary, the error rises again."]},{"text":"So the peaks in error mark **word boundaries**. That is why word-like organization appears without any instruction to divide the input.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p024":[{"text":"A similar model trained on **simple sentences** begins to identify **grammatical classes**. It even picks up some **semantic information**.","children":[]},{"text":"The model's task is still to predict the next word.","children":["The categories appear as a **side effect** of learning to predict sequences.","No one told the model which words are nouns or verbs."]},{"text":"Elman examined the **hidden-unit activation patterns** for each word.","children":["Words used in similar contexts developed similar patterns.","Similar patterns cluster together."]},{"text":"The clusters follow grammar and meaning.","children":["**Nouns** and **verbs** form separate broad groups.","Nouns split into **animates** and **inanimates**.","Animates split further into **humans** and **animals**.","Inanimates include groups for **food** and **breakables**.","Verbs split by how they take a **direct object**."]},{"text":"So knowing a word's class helps predict what can follow it. That is why prediction training produces grammatical and semantic grouping.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p025":[{"text":"Many engineering solutions in **Natural Language Processing** work the same way. **Massive corpora** are analyzed statistically to find patterns. Corpora are collections of texts and transcriptions of spoken language.","children":[]},{"text":"**Peter Norvig** discussed Chomsky's critique.","children":["He agreed that neither sentence appeared in the published literature before 1955.","But every two-word part had occurred before.","\"Colorless green\" appeared in a 1942 book.","\"Green ideas\" appeared in The New Republic in 1922.","\"Ideas sleep in books\" appeared in Current Opinion in 1912."]},{"text":"**Pereira (2001)** tested a statistical model on the two sentences.","children":["The model was a **finite-state model** augmented with **word categories**.","It was trained by **expectation maximization** on **newspaper text**.","It computed that sentence (a) is **200,000 times more probable** than sentence (b)."]},{"text":"Both sentences use the same words.","children":["So the difference comes from how the words and their categories are arranged."]},{"text":"So a statistical model can tell the two sentences apart. That challenges the claim that new sentences are beyond statistical learning.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p026":[],"m1-lecture06-p027":[{"text":"Psycholinguistics has two important traditions. They differ in what linguistic knowledge is assumed to be.","children":[]},{"text":"The first tradition is **GOFAI**, **modular** or **Chomskyan**.","children":["It assumes linguistic knowledge is essentially **abstract and symbolic**.","It assumes linguistic representations are **different in kind** from other knowledge.","This knowledge still has to **interface** with messy perceptual, motor, memory and other cognitive capacities.","The psycholinguist's job is to study this interface."]},{"text":"The second tradition is **emergentist** or **functionalist**.","children":["It assumes symbol and rule-like behavior are **emergent properties** of a **statistical learning** process.","It assumes perceptual, motor and cognitive processes actually **shape** linguistic representations.","The psycholinguist's job is to study **language use**. It relates language use to more general psychological phenomena."]},{"text":"So the traditions disagree about where regularities come from. One says a distinct abstract system. The other says the processes that learn and use language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p028":[],"m1-lecture06-p029":[],"m1-lecture06-p030":[{"text":"The **emergentist** or **statistical learning** approach starts from the messiness of language. The **\"i before e\" rule** has exceptions, and that messiness is all over every language.","children":[]},{"text":"Each level of language shows it.","children":["**Phonetic categories** are probabilistically defined over multiple dimensions.","**Words** are ambiguous in every way imaginable.","**Morphological paradigms** have exceptions. \"Add -ed\" forms the English past tense, but RUN-RAN and SLEEP-SLEPT break it.","**Syntactic rules** are open to dialect and individual variation. They are also open to innovation and change over time."]},{"text":"A strong pattern and its exceptions exist side by side.","children":["Adding -ed works for many verbs.","Irregular forms remain part of what speakers know."]},{"text":"So no single fixed rule covers each level. That is why this approach lets experience build graded expectations.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p031":[{"text":"**Moravec's paradox** is a problem for the **GOFAI** approach. It says the tasks hard for computers are the opposite of those hard for humans.","children":[]},{"text":"For **computers**, proving theorems and solving geometry problems is comparatively easy.","children":["A supposedly simple task is extremely difficult. Recognizing a face is one such task.","Crossing a room without bumping into anything is another."]},{"text":"For **humans**, the opposite is true.","children":["Recognizing faces and walking across rooms feel easy.","Proving theorems feels hard."]},{"text":"The paradox raises a question about language.","children":["Characterizing language in formal terms, as for a computer program, may be a **false trail**.","Perceptual and motor processing may matter more than formal rules suggest."]},{"text":"So formal problem solving may not be a complete model of intelligence or of language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p032":[{"text":"Fixed categories and rules are often **fuzzy** in practice. This has been recognized for a long time.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wittgenstein (1953)** made the point in **Philosophical Investigations**.","children":["His example is the category of **games**.","Board games, card games, ball games and Olympic games are all called games.","When you look at them, no single property belongs to every one."]},{"text":"These members are held together by **family resemblance**.","children":["One pair of games shares a feature.","Another pair shares a different feature.","The similarities overlap without one defining feature."]},{"text":"This challenges strict definitions.","children":["A useful category does not need one list of necessary properties."]},{"text":"So categories can stay coherent even when their members differ. That is why fixed rules often turn out to be fuzzy.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p033":[{"text":"Many phenomena in language are **probabilistic**. Wittgenstein's games make the same point about categories.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wittgenstein (1953)** compared different games in **Philosophical Investigations**.","children":["Some games involve **amusement**. Some involve **winning and losing** or **competition** between players. Others involve **skill** or **luck**.","These features do not occur in every game in the same combination."]},{"text":"The comparison changes from game to game.","children":["For **amusement**, chess is compared with noughts and crosses.","For **competition**, patience is a game with no opponent.","In **ball games** there is winning and losing. When a child throws a ball at the wall and catches it again, that feature disappears.","For **skill**, skill in chess differs from skill in tennis.","**Ring-a-ring-a-roses** keeps amusement, but many other features disappear."]},{"text":"Features that seemed central in one game disappear in another.","children":["Similarities crop up and disappear.","The result is a complicated network of similarities **overlapping and criss-crossing**.","Some are overall similarities, and some are similarities of detail."]},{"text":"So a category can rest on graded similarity rather than a fixed definition. That is why many phenomena in language are probabilistic.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p034":[{"text":"**Family resemblances**, **similarity-based generalization** and **hunches** challenged formal accounts of the mind. Many psychologists produced results of this kind.","children":[]},{"text":"Each researcher worked in a different area.","children":["**Roger Shepard** studied **perception**.","**Eleanor Rosch** studied **categorization**.","**Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky** studied **decision-making**.","Many others did similar work."]},{"text":"Their results made a lot of cognition seem less suited to **formal, rational interpretation**.","children":["People relied on similarity and graded structure.","Their judgments did not follow a simple idealized rational procedure."]},{"text":"These areas point the same way.","children":["Language may also reflect similarity-based generalization.","It may reflect properties of actual processing."]},{"text":"So psychology as a whole moved toward explanations based on real representations and processing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p001":[],"m1-lecture07-p002":[{"text":"**Speech errors** are slips in which a speaker produces something other than the intended utterance. They reveal how an utterance is planned.","children":[]},{"text":"People make speech errors because many items are active at once during planning.","children":["**Noise**, **frequency**, other active material and **similarity** can each help the wrong item win."]},{"text":"Speech errors interest psycholinguists because each error has a structure.","children":["An error can split a word into its parts.","An error can move a sound into another position.","An error can replace the intended word with a related word.","The form of the error gives evidence about the units the production system uses."]},{"text":"**Fromkin** titled the article \"Speech errors as linguistic evidence.\"","children":["Some linguists study **competence** (knowledge of the language) apart from **performance** (actual speaking).","From that view, an error is a disruption of performance, so it can be set aside.","Fromkin instead treats the structure of errors as evidence about the language system. That is why the title is gutsy."]},{"text":"So a useful error is one that is compared with the intended form. The parts that changed and the parts that stayed reveal what the system was doing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p003":[{"text":"**Spreading activation** models explain how an unintended item can become available during speech planning. Activation spreads from each word to related items.","children":[]},{"text":"Four key concepts explain how speech errors happen in these models.","children":["**Noise** is not only literal acoustic noise, although that can cause problems too. It is also fluctuation and interference in activation in different parts of the system.","**Frequency** affects how easily an item becomes active. Common words are easier to activate than less common words. This effect seems to reach many levels of speech production.","**Other sources of activation** add competitors. What a speaker just said stays active. What the speaker plans to say is already active. Things the speaker is thinking about but trying not to say may also be active.","**Similarity** lets activation spread more easily between related items. The similarity can be semantic, syntactic or phonological."]},{"text":"So the intended item competes with alternatives that get activation from several sources at once. A speech error happens when one of those competitors wins.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p004":[{"text":"A **tip-of-the-tongue (TOT) state** is partial activation of a word's meaning and sound form. That activation falls short of the **threshold** for producing the word.","children":[]},{"text":"The person knows the meaning but cannot retrieve enough of the sound form to say it.","children":["Less frequent words are more susceptible to TOT states. Their forms are harder to activate, so they reach the threshold less easily."]},{"text":"A **prime** (a related word read just before) can help.","children":["The prime adds activation to the missing sound form.","That extra activation can push the form over the threshold."]},{"text":"**Lise Abrams** reviewed an example in which a definition asked for goods traded illegally. The target word was \"contraband.\"","children":["People in a TOT state read a prime word and then tried the target again.","**For first-syllable primes** such as \"contact,\" \"connotation\" and \"conscious,\" about **50 percent** of TOT states were resolved.","**For middle-syllable primes**, resolution was intermediate.","**For final-syllable primes**, resolution was close to the unrelated condition.","**For unrelated words**, only about one quarter were resolved."]},{"text":"So priming helps most when the overlap is at the start of the word.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p005":[{"text":"**Slips of the tongue** differ in the direction a sound moves. Spooner supposedly once said \"Mardon me padam, this pie is occupewed. Can I sew you to another sheet?\"","children":[]},{"text":"These examples come from Fromkin. Each type compares the intended form with the produced form.","children":["**In anticipation**, a sound planned for later appears too soon. \"Week long race\" became \"reek long race\" because the upcoming r replaced the start of \"week.\"","**In perseveration**, an earlier sound carries into a later position. \"John gave the boy\" became \"John gave the goy\" because the earlier g carried into \"boy.\"","**In metathesis**, also called an **exchange**, two elements trade positions. \"Keep a tape\" became \"teep a cape\" because the first k and t swapped. The rest of each word stayed in place."]},{"text":"The type depends on how the two forms relate. It does not depend on whether the result makes sense.","children":[]},{"text":"So the order of the intended sounds tells which type of slip occurred.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p006":[{"text":"**Other sources of activation** are most obviously at work in these slips. What a speaker just said and plans to say stay active during speech.","children":[]},{"text":"Each direction of slip points to a different source of activation.","children":["**For anticipation**, material prepared for an upcoming position is already active. In \"reek long race,\" the r is available before its own position.","**For perseveration**, material just spoken is still active. In \"John gave the goy,\" the g keeps its influence after its own position.","**For an exchange**, two elements in the same planned utterance interfere with each other's positions."]},{"text":"The other concepts also play a part.","children":["**Similarity** and **frequency** affect which candidates compete well.","**Noise** (fluctuation in activation) can affect which candidate gets selected."]},{"text":"Planning reaches beyond the sound being said right now. This overlap lets a speaker prepare what comes next. That is why the wrong active element can sometimes fill a position.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p007":[{"text":"A **mondegreen** is a **slip of the ear**, in which a listener mishears what was said. The listener groups the sounds into different words than the speaker intended.","children":[]},{"text":"The term itself comes from a mishearing.","children":["\"Laid him on the green\" was heard as \"Lady Mondegreen.\"","In a song, \"Hold me closer, tiny dancer\" is misheard as \"Hold me closer, Tony Danza.\" The real words are \"Tiny Dancer,\" like the title of the song.","**Andrew Nevins** is analyzing crowdsourced mondegreens from kissthisguy.com."]},{"text":"Speech is ambiguous because it arrives as a continuous stream. So listeners must infer the **word boundaries**.","children":["\"The good candy came anyways\" and \"The good can decay many ways\" sound very similar.","A listener tells them apart mainly by **context**."]},{"text":"Context matters most in songs. In songs, **prosody** (the rhythm and melody of speech) is less helpful. So listeners lean on context and familiar words, and sometimes they pick the wrong ones.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p008":[{"text":"A **malapropism** of this kind is a **perceptual error** more than a speech error. The speaker intends to say exactly what they said.","children":[]},{"text":"The error starts when the speaker hears an unfamiliar word.","children":["The speaker hears a word like \"congenital,\" which means present from birth.","The speaker decides that the intended word must have been a more familiar word, such as \"congenial.\"","Later the speaker describes \"a congenial heart defect\" and means it."]},{"text":"This is **top-down processing** (using known words and expectations to interpret what is heard).","children":["A familiar word helps organize the heard sound.","The familiar word wins over the less familiar target."]},{"text":"Other collected examples include \"lettuce days,\" \"decaffeinated him\" and \"Utopian tubes tied.\"","children":[]},{"text":"So a production slip and a mislearned expression can sound equally odd. They arise through different processes.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p009":[{"text":"**Fromkin** used speech errors as data about the units of **speech planning**. At the time, researchers argued about whether the **phoneme** (or phone, a single speech sound) was a unit in planning.","children":[]},{"text":"One possibility was that whole consonant **clusters** always move together.","children":["Then errors would have looked like \"gish frotto\" or \"clesh freer water.\""]},{"text":"Instead, Fromkin presents evidence that clusters get broken up all the time in production.","children":["**For \"fish grotto,\"** the error was \"frish gotto.\" The r left the gr cluster and joined the f in the first word.","**For \"fresh clear water,\"** the error was \"flesh queer water.\" The r and the l exchanged. The first consonants f and k stayed in place.","**For \"split pea soup,\"** the error was \"plit spea soup.\" Only the s moved out of the cluster."]},{"text":"So single segments can move on their own. That is why the phoneme counts as a unit in speech planning.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p010":[{"text":"**Fromkin (1971)** proposed an early version of the production model that Menn describes. The model explains how an intended meaning becomes an utterance.","children":[]},{"text":"The model runs through a series of stages.","children":["It starts with the **meaning to be conveyed**.","A syntactic structure generator and a semantic feature generator build **syntactic-semantic structures**.","An intonation contour generator adds **primary stress** and **intonation**.","The **lexicon** then supplies words specified for their features and the order of their segments.","This gives strings of segments divided into syllables.","**Morphophonemic rules** and **phonetic rules** then fully specify the sound sequence.","**Motor commands** to the muscles finally produce the **utterance**."]},{"text":"Each stage is a separate representation. So an error can affect one stage while the other stages stay organized. That is why different kinds of errors give evidence about different stages.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p011":[{"text":"The **basic model** of speech production has five levels. Each level solves a different problem in producing an utterance.","children":[]},{"text":"The levels come in a fixed order.","children":["The **message level** holds what the speaker intends to convey.","The **functional level** selects **lemmas** (word identities before their final form) and links them to **semantic roles**.","The **positional level** arranges them into a clause.","The **phonological encoding level** specifies the sound structure.","The **speech gesture level** prepares the movements used to say it."]},{"text":"Following Menn, the message and functional levels come first. The sound slips such as \"reek long race\" were already phonological encoding errors.","children":[]},{"text":"Separating the levels matters because the everyday word \"word\" blurs them together. Choosing a fitting word is different from choosing its grammatical form. It is also different from assembling its sounds. So different errors can be traced to different levels.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p012":[{"text":"The **message level** decides what information a speaker intends to convey. The same event can become different messages.","children":[]},{"text":"Take the sentence \"I won a bunch of money on the game Sunday.\"","children":["**For a fan of the losing team**, the listener's feelings may affect how directly the speaker stresses the win.","**For someone not interested in football**, the speaker may need to explain why the event matters.","**For a tweet**, the short public post changes which details are included."]},{"text":"The event stays the same in each case. The purpose and the chosen information change with the audience.","children":[]},{"text":"The message level is mysterious and hard to investigate experimentally. Giving speakers the same event does not mean they all intend the same content. So the message depends on the audience, the goals and what the listener already knows.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p013":[{"text":"Some speech production studies try to control the message level with an **elicitation paradigm** (a task built to draw out speech). Speakers describe the same picture book, such as \"Frog, where are you?\"","children":[]},{"text":"The picture book gives every speaker the same scene.","children":["The scene contains a boy, a dog and frogs.","Researchers can compare descriptions because the visible material is held constant."]},{"text":"The intended message is still hard to define.","children":["One speaker may focus on the boy, and another on the dog or the frogs.","Speakers may assume different knowledge in the audience.","Age, culture and audience may change how the message is built."]},{"text":"So the method controls what speakers see. It does not fully control what they intend to say.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p014":[{"text":"A **lemma** is a word's identity before its final grammatical form is set. The term is borrowed from mathematics, where it refers to a proposition in an argument.","children":[]},{"text":"Selecting whole \"words\" at the functional level cannot be right. One error explains why.","children":["The intended sentence was \"Where'd I put it when I brought it in.\"","The produced sentence was \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in.\""]},{"text":"The verbs switched places, but the moved verb changed tense.","children":["\"Where'd\" already contains the past-tense helper did. A verb after did takes the base form.","So the moved verb came out as \"bring,\" not \"brought.\"","If the word \"brought\" had been selected, it would have moved unchanged.","PUT looks the same in both forms, so BRING gives the visible evidence."]},{"text":"Instead, the lemma BRING was selected and moved. Its tense was applied later, in its new position. That is why the functional level selects lemmas rather than finished words.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p015":[{"text":"An **error in lemma selection** can replace the intended word with its **opposite**. People do this often because opposites are closely related in the lexical system.","children":[]},{"text":"Each example keeps the sentence and swaps one word.","children":["**For like and hate**, a speaker said \"I really like to\" and corrected it to hate.","**For bottom and top**, a speaker said bottom and meant the top of the stack of books.","**For hot and cold**, a speaker said \"This room is too damn hot\" and corrected it to cold.","**For oral and written**, a speaker said oral and meant the written part of the exam."]},{"text":"Each pair shares a domain, such as temperature, position or exam format. The wrong word still fits the sentence. So the sentence stays well formed while its meaning is reversed.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p016":[{"text":"**Antonyms** (words with opposite meanings) are actually quite similar at several levels. So activation spreads easily from a lemma to its antonym.","children":[]},{"text":"Two kinds of overlap give the antonym strong activation.","children":["Antonyms are **semantically related**. They share a domain, such as temperature for hot and cold.","Antonyms are **strongly associated**. Thinking of one brings the other into the same comparison.","In the spreading activation framework, these overlaps add up to a perfect storm."]},{"text":"Two more kinds of overlap let the antonym pass through.","children":["Antonyms can take on the **same semantic roles**.","Antonyms belong to the **same word class**, so they can take on the same syntactic roles.","So the rest of the speech production system can handle the wrong word."]},{"text":"That is why a big change in meaning can come from a small, grammatical substitution.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p017":[{"text":"A **blend** combines parts of two competing words into one produced form. **Lewis Carroll** made blends on purpose and called them portmanteau words.","children":[]},{"text":"Carroll explained his word \"frumious\" in the preface to The Hunting of the Snark.","children":["A speaker means to say both \"fuming\" and \"furious\" but leaves the order unsettled.","Leaning slightly toward fuming gives \"fuming-furious.\"","Leaning slightly toward furious gives \"furious-fuming.\"","A perfectly balanced mind gives \"frumious.\""]},{"text":"**Fromkin** quoted Carroll because he said blends are possible in natural speech. He also proposed a hypothesis about how they occur.","children":[]},{"text":"Fromkin noted that such complete blends are seldom found in normal speech.","children":["A blend of [fjurijəs] and [fjumiŋ] is more likely to come out as [fjumijəs] or [fjuriŋ].","This happens because the first syllables are identical."]},{"text":"So spontaneous blends tend to join the two words where their sounds overlap.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p018":[{"text":"**Spontaneous blends** reveal competition among alternative ways to express a message. Each produced form mixes two candidate words.","children":[]},{"text":"Each blend comes from a pair of competing words.","children":["\"I swindged\" blends **switch** and **changed**.","Other blends mix **mainly** and **mostly**, or **bother** and **trouble**.","Others mix **swinging** and **hip**, **spirant** and **fricative**, or **player** and **athlete**."]},{"text":"**Fromkin** proposed how blends happen.","children":["A speaker has a meaning to convey and selects words by matching **semantic features**.","Where there are several alternatives, the speaker brings both into a **buffer** (a temporary storage compartment) with their sound specifications.","Then either one word is selected, or the two words are blended.","These errors seem to support Carroll's assumptions. That is why Carroll would have been an awesome linguist."]},{"text":"Speech production nearly always has a number of alternative possibilities. Choosing among them is an ordinary part of speaking. So blends make that everyday competition visible.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p019":[{"text":"**Semantic roles** (also called functional roles) describe how each participant relates to an event. The roles stay the same when the sentence form changes.","children":[]},{"text":"The same event can be said two ways.","children":["**In the passive** \"The plums in the icebox were eaten by me,\" the order is Undergoer, Location, Verb, Agent.","**In the active** \"I have eaten the plums in the icebox,\" the order is Agent, Verb, Undergoer, Location."]},{"text":"At the functional level, lemmas get slotted into these roles.","children":["The verb is TO EAT.","The **agent** (the one doing the action) is ME.","The **undergoer** (the thing the action happens to) is PLUM.","The icebox is the **location**."]},{"text":"The order of the roles has syntactic consequences.","children":["The lemma ME becomes \"I\" in the active sentence.","The lemma ME becomes \"me\" after \"by\" in the passive sentence.","This choice of word form happens later, at the **positional level**."]},{"text":"So the event stays fixed while its sentence form changes.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p020":[{"text":"At the **functional level**, a lot of planning is already done. Yet the speaker is nowhere near putting a clause together, much less saying it out loud.","children":[]},{"text":"Three things are done by the end of this level.","children":["The speaker has decided on a **message** to convey.","The speaker has selected appropriate **lemmas**.","The speaker has linked the lemmas with **semantic roles**."]},{"text":"Much is still undecided.","children":["For an eating event, the plan has EAT, the one eating and the thing eaten.","It has not yet decided whether the sentence is active or passive.","It has not yet set how the pronouns will be expressed.","It has not yet set the full sound sequence."]},{"text":"So later levels still have to order the lemmas and turn them into speech.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p021":[],"m1-lecture07-p022":[],"m1-lecture07-p023":[{"text":"Very often **speech errors go undetected**. **Top-down effects** in speech perception explain this.","children":[]},{"text":"Listeners use context and expectations to recover the intended message.","children":["So a speaker's real error can pass without notice.","A similar process is assumed for many malapropisms and mondegreens."]},{"text":"Some \"errors\" are made on purpose for stylistic reasons.","children":["**Paul Krugman** wrote that \"the upper hand is on the other foot,\" quoting Hot Shots Part Deux.","The phrase combines \"the upper hand\" with the idea that the shoe is on the other foot."]},{"text":"A deliberate error can be **more memorable** than its correct counterpart.","children":["The odd mismatch draws attention to the wording itself.","The listener recognizes both familiar phrases, so the mix is funny."]},{"text":"So whether an error was intentional matters when it is used as evidence about speech production.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p001":[],"m1-lecture08-p002":[{"text":"A **speech production model** describes how a speaker goes from a message to spoken words. The lower three levels finish the job after the functional level.","children":[]},{"text":"Each lower level has its own task.","children":["At the **positional level**, the chosen words are placed into a grammatical structure.","At the **phonological encoding level**, the words become a string of **phonemes** (speech sounds that tell words apart).","At the **speech gestural level**, the motor system turns that string into movements."]},{"text":"Speech errors give evidence for **abstract units**.","children":["Units such as **phonemes**, **lemmas** and **noun phrases** cannot be heard directly.","Errors move these units or leave them in place in regular ways. That is why the units are useful in a theory of speaking."]},{"text":"Collected errors can be sorted by the level where they happened. Purely **observational research** also has weaknesses. So **experimental methods** are needed as well.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p003":[{"text":"The sentence \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in?\" is an error with two verbs. It was earlier evidence that **lemmas** (words chosen for meaning and grammar, before their sounds) are a useful idea.","children":[]},{"text":"The **functional level** settles three facts for this sentence.","children":["There are two verbs, PUT and BRING.","I/ME is the **Agent** (the doer) of both verbs.","IT is the **Undergoer** (the thing acted on) of both verbs."]},{"text":"The functional level still leaves other facts open.","children":["It does not set the **temporal relationship** between the two verbs, which the word when expresses.","It does not build the question or choose the tense of each verb."]},{"text":"The **positional level** expresses all of these facts in syntax. So word order, the question form and the verb forms are set there.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p004":[{"text":"The **positional level** builds a syntactic structure with **slots** for the lemmas. Without the question-forming steps, the frame is roughly \"Where'd I [verb, root form] it when I [verb, past] it in?\"","children":[]},{"text":"Each slot asks for its own verb form.","children":["**For the first clause**, the slot needs the **base form** of the verb. Getting this clause right took real syntax, because it has **Wh- movement** and an **auxiliary verb**.","**For the when clause**, the slot needs the **past form**.","The frame also holds the other **particles** the grammar requires."]},{"text":"The lemmas from the functional level are then fed into these slots. The error arises during this filling.","children":["BRING went into the root form slot, so it came out as bring.","PUT went into the past slot, so it came out as put."]},{"text":"So the verbs landed in the wrong slots, but each took the form its slot required.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p005":[{"text":"The error \"a language needer learns\" is like the bring and put error in one way. It differs in another way.","children":[]},{"text":"The intended phrase is \"a language learner needs.\"","children":["**The similarity** is that two lemmas, LEARN and NEED, got **switched** while being fed into the positional level.","The positional frame stayed mostly the same. It is still a noun phrase followed by a verb."]},{"text":"**The difference** is in the noun.","children":["The noun is actually a **verb plus an affix**.","In learner, the verb learn carries the ending -er. In the error, need carries that ending instead, giving needer.","Adding -er to a verb is **derivational morphology** (making a new word from an existing one)."]},{"text":"So this error swaps roots inside words, not just whole words.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p006":[{"text":"In \"a language needer learns,\" two lemmas switched while the **positional level** stayed mostly the same. The interesting part is the noun.","children":[]},{"text":"The noun slot is built as a **verb plus -er**.","children":["**-er** is a **derivational affix** (an ending that makes a new word). Here it turns a verb into a noun for the doer, as in learner.","The verb slot asks for the **third person singular** form. Its ending -s is **inflectional morphology** (an ending set by grammar)."]},{"text":"The tree has a noun phrase (NP) and a verb phrase (VP).","children":["Inside the NP, the noun position is a verb plus -er.","Inside the VP, the verb position is a third person singular verb."]},{"text":"The roots NEED and LEARN swapped slots. The endings stayed with their slots.","children":["So need got -er and became needer.","Learn got -s and became learns."]},{"text":"If the whole words learner and needs had swapped, the endings would have moved too. That is why the affixes count as **stranded** in their positions.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p007":[{"text":"The error \"He did that easy enoughly\" should have been \"He did that easily enough.\" It raises the same two questions as \"a language needer learns.\"","children":[]},{"text":"In the intended phrase, the ending **-ly** attaches to easy.","children":["**In the error**, -ly attaches to enough instead.","So easy loses its ending, and the odd form enoughly appears."]},{"text":"The two questions ask how the errors compare.","children":["One question is how this error is **similar** to \"a language needer learns.\"","The other is how it is **different**."]},{"text":"Both answers involve where the affix went and how the two words were arranged. So the intended and produced forms must be compared word by word.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p008":[{"text":"\"He did that easy enoughly\" and \"a language needer learns\" share a **stranded affix** (an ending that stays in its slot while the root changes).","children":[]},{"text":"The shared part is the affix.","children":["**-ly** can make an **adjective** into an **adverb**.","In both errors, an ending sits on the wrong root."]},{"text":"The difference is in the frame.","children":["In \"needer,\" the **positional frame** stayed the same.","In \"enoughly,\" something also went wrong **while setting up the positional level**."]},{"text":"Two trees compare the frames. Each has an adjective phrase (AP) on top.","children":["**In the intended frame**, the adverb slot comes first. It is built as an adjective plus -ly. The plain adjective slot comes second.","**In the starred error frame**, the adjective slot comes first and the adverb slot comes second.","Easy filled the first slot, and enough filled the second slot. So enough got the -ly."]},{"text":"That is why this error points to a mistake in the frame itself, not only in filling it.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p009":[{"text":"Errors like needer and enoughly are evidence that **derivational morphology** is real. **Affixes** seem able to float around at the **positional level**.","children":[]},{"text":"Another view says derived words are just separate stored words.","children":["In that view, they link to their roots in some rule-like way.","The lexicon would hold sets such as needy, need and needless, or unlearn, learn and learner.","Then affixes would need no separate representation. Speakers would not need quirky rules for building common words."]},{"text":"Errors argue against that view. Speakers make **non-words** like \"needer\" and \"enoughly\" on the fly. These words cannot come from a stored list. So the errors suggest three things.","children":["**Lemmas and affixes** are somehow represented **separately**.","**Affixes have a slot** in the positional level.","**Derived words** can be produced by a **grammatical process**."]},{"text":"So the speaker builds some words while speaking, not only retrieves them.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p010":[{"text":"Adding an affix at the **positional level** still leaves **phonological work** to do. The sounds of the new word must still be worked out.","children":[]},{"text":"Simple cases add sounds to the end.","children":["Easy /’i.zi/ becomes easily /’i.zIl.i/.","Enough /i.’nəf/ becomes enoughly /i.’nəf.li/."]},{"text":"Other cases change the base itself.","children":["The word certificate is pronounced /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/.","The word certification is pronounced /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/.","The word recertification is pronounced /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/.","Across these forms, **vowels change** and **stress moves**."]},{"text":"The positional level only states a process like adding -er or making an adjective into an adverb. That process does not give the final sounds. So a later level must still turn the plan into the right pronunciation.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p011":[{"text":"**Phonological encoding** turns lemmas, affixes and syntactic particles into a **string of phonemes** to produce.","children":[]},{"text":"For \"easy enoughly,\" this level worked fine.","children":["The speaker did not say \"easy /inəvɪli/.\"","The speaker made no other error in the sequence of phonemes.","So the error happened earlier, at the positional level."]},{"text":"Other errors do happen at this level.","children":["For **fish grotto**, the speaker said gish frotto.","For **Chomsky and Halle**, the speaker said Chomsky and Challe.","For **with this ring I do wed**, the speaker said with this wing I do red."]},{"text":"In each case, the problem is the **ordering of phonemes**.","children":["Menn describes these errors as **\"neat.\"**","They follow the **phonotactic patterns** (rules for allowed sound sequences) of English.","As far as anyone knows, they are also **clearly produced**."]},{"text":"So a phonological encoding error gives a clean wrong sound sequence.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p012":[{"text":"The **speech gestural level** turns the planned phonemes into **gestures** carried out by the **motor system**.","children":[]},{"text":"Errors here differ from phonological encoding errors.","children":["They are **not necessarily \"neat.\"**","They result in **unclear or imprecise speech**."]},{"text":"**Many errors are ambiguous**.","children":["Ad hoc came out as odd hack. In sounds, /æd hɑk/ became /ɑd hæk/.","/æ/ is a low front vowel, as in bat. /ɑ/ is a low back vowel, as in hot."]},{"text":"Two accounts fit this error.","children":["**At the phonological encoding level**, it could be **metathesis** (two sounds switching places in the plan).","**At the gestural level**, it could be a **miscuing of tongue position** during speaking."]},{"text":"So the same error could come from the plan or from the movements.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p013":[],"m1-lecture08-p014":[{"text":"Each collected error has to be placed at the level where it happened. Some are about word forms, and some are about sounds.","children":[]},{"text":"Two examples involve word forms.","children":["**For buyed in**, the speaker meant bought in. Buyed applies the regular past ending to a verb with an irregular past. This is **morphological regularization**.","**For arising**, the speaker said \"arriving.\" One consonant changed in a closely similar word."]},{"text":"Two examples involve sounds.","children":["**For pretty close to here**, close was said as [kloʊz]. The final consonant became voiced, unlike the voiceless one expected for this close.","**For thoroughly**, the speaker said \"throfly.\" Several parts of the sound sequence changed."]},{"text":"So each error is analyzed by comparing the intended sounds with the produced sounds. Then the changed unit points to a level.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p015":[{"text":"More collected errors move different units. Some move whole words, and some move single sounds.","children":[]},{"text":"Some errors move words.","children":["In \"Wash my teeth and brush my face,\" the **verbs** wash and brush switched. Their objects stayed in place."]},{"text":"Some errors move sounds.","children":["**For following vowel**, the speaker said \"vollowing fowel.\" The first consonants **switched**.","**For seat heaters**, the speaker said \"heat seaters.\" Sounds moved, but the plural ending stayed on seaters.","**For speech-language pathology**, the speaker said \"speech plath... speech plang.\" The attempts and repairs are part of the record."]},{"text":"Some errors bring in a familiar sequence.","children":["**For Lana del Rey**, the speaker said \"Marina del Rey.\"","\"For all intensive purposes\" replaces part of a familiar phrase with different words.","\"It's in the stimulus\" is another collected error."]},{"text":"So each error has to be checked for which unit moved and which stayed.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p016":[],"m1-lecture08-p017":[{"text":"The speaker said \"you enter a hundred-dollar room and you do yoga.\" The talk was about **hot yoga**.","children":[]},{"text":"The context is what makes it an error.","children":["In a talk about cost, a hundred-dollar room would be normal.","In a talk about hot yoga, the hundred could describe the room's heat, not its price.","So dollar is a real word, but it does not fit the meaning intended."]},{"text":"The sentence is otherwise fine.","children":["The clause is **grammatical**.","The words are easy to pronounce."]},{"text":"So the error lies in **choosing the word** for the message, not in the sounds or movements.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p018":[{"text":"The speaker said \"Law and Order SUV.\" The intended title is Law and Order SVU.","children":[]},{"text":"The two forms use the same three letters.","children":["**SVU** belongs to the show's title.","**SUV** is a familiar vehicle abbreviation.","The last two letters **switched order**."]},{"text":"The produced form is a real, familiar sequence.","children":["So a competing familiar form may have been **selected**.","The switch should not be assumed to be a simple motor slip.","The result is still easy to say."]},{"text":"So the analysis starts with the exact change, SVU becoming SUV. It then asks which familiar forms were competing.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p019":[{"text":"The speaker said \"He cooked his kitchen.\" The intended word was chicken.","children":[]},{"text":"Kitchen and chicken are related in two ways.","children":["**In sound**, the words are very similar. Their first and middle consonants appear in opposite positions.","**In meaning**, both relate to cooking."]},{"text":"The grammar stayed intact.","children":["Both words are nouns.","Both fit the slot after his.","So the sentence keeps its **grammatical frame**."]},{"text":"So this error mixes **sound similarity** with **meaning context**. The analysis has to look at both the word forms and the situation.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p020":[{"text":"**Observational studies** of speech errors depend on what the observer notices and reports.","children":[]},{"text":"The observer's **expectations** matter.","children":["What the observer listens for decides which errors stand out.","Learning about speech production can change those expectations.","So expectations can affect both what is heard and what is reported."]},{"text":"Placing an error in the model also needs **context**.","children":["The analyst must reconstruct the speaker's intended message.","The analyst must also know the situation in which the error happened.","A form can be fine alone but wrong for what the speaker meant."]},{"text":"So a collection of errors reflects the observer as well as the speaker.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p021":[{"text":"**Speech perception** biases which errors observers report.","children":[]},{"text":"Listeners have three biases.","children":["They are not very good at hearing sounds **\"halfway\" between two phonemes**, especially for **consonants**.","They are **biased against** hearing **phonotactically illegal strings** (sound sequences the language does not allow).","They are **biased toward** hearing ambiguous sounds **as words**. A sound partway between \"giss\" and \"kiss\" sounds more like \"kiss.\""]},{"text":"Philosophers of science call this **theory-dependent observation**.","children":["Because of how perception works, observers might miss many errors.","They might even miss **whole classes of errors**."]},{"text":"**Experimental methods** and careful measurement can fix some of these problems. They cannot fix all of them. So reported errors are not a full record of the errors people make.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p022":[],"m1-lecture08-p023":[],"m1-lecture09-p001":[],"m1-lecture09-p002":[{"text":"**Oldfield and Wingfield (1965)** asked people to name pictures of objects. They measured the **response time** for each name.","children":["The example line drawings come from Snodgrass and Vanderwart (1980). That later set of drawings was not used in the original study."]},{"text":"They found a **log-linear relationship** between word frequency and response time.","children":["A log-linear relationship means the times fall on a straight line once frequency is put on a logarithmic scale.","For **frequent names**, naming was faster.","For **rare names**, naming was slower."]},{"text":"The problem is that frequency is related to **word length**, even in this tiny sample of objects.","children":["Frequent words tend to be **shorter**.","So a frequent name and a rare name usually differ in length too.","Either property could cause the difference in naming time."]},{"text":"This problem is called a **confound**.","children":["A confound is a second variable that changes along with the variable being tested.","It gives another possible explanation for the same result."]},{"text":"So the frequency effect is a classic cautionary tale. A clear relationship is only the start of finding out which property causes it. The relationship can be checked with numbers using a web-based tool.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p003":[{"text":"A **lexical database** collects information about many words in one place. Researchers can search it and compare words.","children":[]},{"text":"The **English Lexicon Project** is one such database.","children":["It gives the **lexical characteristics** of words, such as frequency and length.","It also gives **behavioral data** from naming and lexical-decision tasks.","Its homepage has separate links for generating word and nonword lists.","Other links give trial-level data from lexical decision and naming."]},{"text":"The two kinds of links serve two jobs.","children":["One job is choosing words with certain properties.","The other job is looking at how people actually responded to words."]},{"text":"A database lets researchers measure their candidate words instead of trusting intuition.","children":["A list that seems \"frequent\" may also differ in length.","The database reveals that hidden difference before the materials are chosen."]},{"text":"So a lexical database ties the intended manipulation to real measurements of the words.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p004":[{"text":"The word-query tools give **two ways** to get information about words.","children":[]},{"text":"The first way is to **generate a list** of items with specific lexical characteristics.","children":["The researcher sets the properties, and the database returns words that fit.","This helps when a study needs a certain range of frequency or length."]},{"text":"The second way is to **submit a list** of items to retrieve their lexical characteristics.","children":["The researcher already has the words, and the database returns their properties.","This helps when the words were chosen for another reason, such as belonging to a semantic category."]},{"text":"Both ways start from a different place but do the same job.","children":["Each one makes the properties of the stimuli explicit.","Each one can reveal an unintended difference between conditions."]},{"text":"So stimulus design often goes back and forth between searching for words and checking them.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p005":[{"text":"A database query separates **fields to retrieve** from **constraints** on which words get in.","children":[]},{"text":"A field is a property the database reports for each word.","children":["Checkboxes let the researcher request **length** and frequency measures.","Requesting both lets the researcher see whether length changes along with frequency."]},{"text":"A constraint is a limit on a property.","children":["Minimum and maximum boxes set the range for the chosen frequency field.","Only words inside that range enter the list."]},{"text":"For a frequency study, the goal is words that differ in frequency but not in a confound such as length.","children":["The frequency constraints pick out candidate words.","The returned lengths tell the researcher whether more selection is needed."]},{"text":"The researcher turns a design goal into measurable selection criteria. The database does not decide the comparison by itself. So the choice of fields decides which confounds can be checked.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p006":[{"text":"**Word frequency** in English is very uneven. The 200 most frequent words in an old corpus were put into ten \"bins\" of twenty words each.","children":[]},{"text":"The bins reveal two patterns.","children":["For **mean frequency**, the value drops off very dramatically from the most frequent bin to the next.","For **mean length**, the value goes up as frequency goes down."]},{"text":"The steep drop in frequency is **Zipf's law** in action.","children":["Zipf's law describes how a small number of words make up a large share of all usage.","The top twenty words are used far more often than the next twenty."]},{"text":"Length is **inversely related** to frequency.","children":["Inversely related means that as one goes up, the other goes down.","The most frequent bins hold the shortest words on average."]},{"text":"That is why frequency and length are hard to manipulate separately. Picking very frequent words also picks many short words.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p007":[{"text":"A **boxplot** summarizes a whole distribution, not just its average. The line inside the box is the **median**, which is the middle value.","children":[]},{"text":"The comparison used **467 high-frequency (HF) words** and **467 low-frequency (LF) words**.","children":["The HF words were the 467 most frequent words.","The LF words were selected at random."]},{"text":"The two length distributions differ a lot.","children":["For **HF words**, the lengths center on a shorter value.","For **LF words**, the box and the median sit higher.","**Fewer than 25%** of HF words are as long as the median LF word."]},{"text":"The difference covers most of each sample.","children":["It does not come from one unusually short or long word.","The two boxes barely overlap around their medians."]},{"text":"So random sampling does not solve the problem. Choosing LF words at random keeps the natural link between frequency and length in the materials.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p008":[{"text":"The problem is that **HF words are generally shorter** than LF words. So this length difference will always happen if LF words are selected at random.","children":[]},{"text":"The solution is **not to select LF words at random**.","children":["The researcher picks **long-ish HF words**.","The researcher picks **short-ish LF words**.","The two groups then have similar lengths but still differ in frequency."]},{"text":"This is called **matching** on length while manipulating frequency.","children":["The chosen words come from the parts of the two distributions that overlap.","These words are less typical of their groups, but they make the comparison possible."]},{"text":"Matching changes only the experimental sample.","children":["It does not claim that frequency and length are unrelated in the language.","The final groups must be checked to confirm they differ in frequency and agree in length."]},{"text":"So matching lets the frequency effect be tested with length held similar.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p009":[{"text":"**Oldfield and Wingfield** eventually addressed the length confound. They **matched their high- and low-frequency words for length**.","children":[]},{"text":"They still found a **robust frequency effect**.","children":["If length were the whole cause, matching length should have removed the naming difference.","The difference stayed, so length cannot be the whole cause."]},{"text":"They concluded that **frequency effects are not merely length effects in disguise**.","children":["The matched design keeps frequency different and makes length similar.","That separation gives the surviving effect its value as evidence."]},{"text":"This logic runs through much of the field.","children":["A great deal of psycholinguistics is an attempt to show that an observed effect is not just an effect of some **confounding variable**.","A confounding variable is one that changes along with the variable of interest."]},{"text":"So a correlation among word properties becomes evidence only after the confound is controlled.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p010":[{"text":"Careful **stimulus matching** checks many word properties at once. **Cutler (1981)** called this problem \"Making up materials is a confounded nuisance.\"","children":[]},{"text":"**Cortese and Simpson (2000)** did a very good matching job.","children":["They matched regular and irregular words across many properties.","The properties included frequency, familiarity, imageability, length and sound or spelling measures.","Careful matching on a wide range of dimensions is an important part of good stimulus design."]},{"text":"Very fine-grained matching raises **two concerns**.","children":["The first concern is that the chosen items may be somehow \"odd.\" Their statistics may also be inappropriate.","The frequency-length confound is a fact about English and most languages. Picking items to avoid it creates a somewhat **artificial stimulus environment**, even when done well.","The second concern is that **very few items** may be left. This study had only ten words per cell, which is small for a word naming experiment."]},{"text":"So matching trades a cleaner comparison for a stranger and smaller set of words.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p011":[{"text":"Most interesting phenomena in psycholinguistics happen **below the level of conscious awareness**.","children":[]},{"text":"A person naming line drawings does not usually think about word frequency or length.","children":["The person just names the objects.","The frequency and length effects still show up in the naming times."]},{"text":"So psycholinguists do not have to be as crafty about design as some other researchers.","children":["Researchers studying **racial stereotyping** need craftier designs.","Researchers studying **altruism** also need craftier designs.","In naming, people are not aware of the properties being tested."]},{"text":"In **norming studies**, researchers simply ask people to rate words on some dimension.","children":["They just hope for the best.","Some studies still add **fillers**, which are extra items mixed in among the test items."]},{"text":"The question is what the participant is asked to do. It also matters whether that behavior gives the intended evidence. So a simple task can reveal a process people do not notice.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p012":[{"text":"Many tasks measure things that participants do **not consciously control**. These measures serve as **dependent variables**, which are the outcomes a researcher measures.","children":[]},{"text":"Each measure tracks something different.","children":["**Eye-tracking** records where and when people look. It is very revealing in speech production studies.","**EEG** and **fMRI** both measure brain activity.","**Reaction time** and **accuracy** are also unlikely to be under direct control in most experiments.","A **tachistoscope** from the 19th century controlled brief presentation of stimuli."]},{"text":"Brain activity is useful mainly because participants usually cannot control it directly.","children":["It matters less for what it says about the brain itself.","Suppose two kinds of stimuli produce different brain responses with no overt task.","Then those stimuli are different to the participant."]},{"text":"So a good design picks a measure that the participant cannot easily steer. That measure then reveals how the stimuli are processed.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p013":[],"m1-lecture09-p014":[{"text":"**Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005)** studied picture naming with **cyclic semantic blocking**. Participants named sets of objects again and again.","children":[]},{"text":"The sets came in two kinds.","children":["In **homogeneous sets**, the object names were semantically related.","In **heterogeneous sets**, the object names were unrelated."]},{"text":"Naming was slower in related sets once the objects had already been seen.","children":["**Experiment 1** found that the blocking effect appeared after the objects had been named once.","**Experiment 2** showed objects at the same time. It found blocking in naming latency and gaze duration. It found no significant blocking in the gaze-to-speech lag.","**Experiment 3** found that the cost spread to new names from the same categories."]},{"text":"The study has nice design features.","children":["It changes how the objects are presented.","It adds eye tracking.","It adds new items."]},{"text":"So the three experiments together locate where the naming cost arises.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p015":[{"text":"**Roelofs' WEAVER++ model** describes how a single word is produced. It has **conceptual**, **syntactic** and **word-form** levels.","children":[]},{"text":"A general **sentence-production model** has more levels.","children":["It has a message level, a functional level and a positional level.","Phonological encoding and articulation come after those."]},{"text":"The two models do not align exactly.","children":["WEAVER++ focuses on activation for single words.","Its syntactic information is not \"positional.\"","Instead, it holds single-word features such as number and gender."]},{"text":"The conceptual level and the **lemma** level of WEAVER++ matter most for semantic blocking.","children":["A lemma is a word candidate with its syntactic information, such as number and gender.","Activation of a concept spreads to its lemma.","Word-form encoding comes later, after a lemma is selected."]},{"text":"So the comparison places the competition before the sounds and movements are planned.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p016":[{"text":"A **spreading-activation model** links related concepts. When one concept is active, activation spreads to its neighbors.","children":[]},{"text":"Activating **duck** also activates shared features such as animal, bird and swim.","children":["Those features connect to other animals such as **fish**, **snake** and **mouse**.","Each concept passes activation down to its own lemma."]},{"text":"A naive view of spreading activation makes one prediction.","children":["A whole block of similar pictures would make each name more active.","So naming times should get faster."]},{"text":"The model reveals how naming can instead get **slower**.","children":["Naming requires choosing one candidate over the others.","A related set makes the target more available.","It also makes the competitors more available."]},{"text":"So naming time depends on the selection process, not only on how active the target is.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p017":[{"text":"Speech production requires telling apart **activation** and **selection**. Activation is how active a word candidate is. Selection is choosing the one lemma to say.","children":[]},{"text":"Spreading activation from **duck** to **fish** means activating one helps activate the other.","children":["This helps make related words available.","This causes problems when the task is to select the correct lemma from many active ones."]},{"text":"**WEAVER++** assumes that selection time depends on a **ratio**.","children":["The top of the ratio is the activity of the most active lemma.","The bottom of the ratio is the overall activation of all lemmas.","A larger total activation makes the ratio smaller, so selection takes longer."]},{"text":"Feedback from the **category level** adds collateral activation.","children":["Collateral activation is extra activation for other items in the same category.","It raises the total activation of all lemmas.","The target becomes less dominant over its competitors."]},{"text":"That is why semantic relatedness can **slow down lemma selection** instead of speeding it up.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p018":[{"text":"Belke and colleagues ran the experiment for **8 cycles**. In each cycle, all of the stimuli were presented.","children":[]},{"text":"The objects appeared in two kinds of blocks.","children":["In **homogeneous (HOM) blocks**, the items were related, such as \"duck\", \"mouse\", \"fish\", \"snake\".","In **heterogeneous (HET) blocks**, the items were unrelated, such as \"duck\", \"saw\", \"chair\", \"bus\"."]},{"text":"The same stimulus appeared in both kinds of block.","children":["For **duck**, it was named in a HOM block in one cycle.","In the next cycle, duck was named in a HET block.","The blocks alternated across cycles."]},{"text":"This design allows two comparisons.","children":["Response times can change with repeated naming across cycles.","Response times can differ between HOM and HET blocks."]},{"text":"So an item belongs to HOM or HET only because of the other objects in its set.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p019":[{"text":"Two things happen in the naming times at once.","children":[]},{"text":"The first is a big **identity priming effect**.","children":["Identity priming is faster naming of an item because it was named before.","Both HOM and HET times drop sharply after the first cycle."]},{"text":"The second is a **semantic blocking effect**.","children":["Semantic blocking is slower naming in related blocks than in unrelated blocks.","In later cycles, HOM responses are slower than HET responses."]},{"text":"The two effects come from different comparisons.","children":["For **identity priming**, the comparison is across cycles for the same item.","For **semantic blocking**, the comparison is HOM against HET within one cycle.","A condition can get faster over time and still be slower than the other condition."]},{"text":"So the two effects do not contradict each other. Mixing up the comparisons would merge them into one misleading description.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p020":[{"text":"A possible confound is **visual similarity**. Related objects might look alike, so the blocking effect could arise at the level of **visual object recognition**.","children":[]},{"text":"Belke and colleagues gave two responses.","children":["First, the stimuli were **normed**. Objects in the same category were not more visually similar on average than objects in different categories.","Second, they ran a **control experiment** that blocked visual similarity."]},{"text":"The control experiment found a different result.","children":["For **visual similarity**, the blocking effect appeared in the first block of trials.","For **semantic blocking** in Experiment 1, the effect appeared only after the first cycle."]},{"text":"The two responses do different jobs.","children":["Norming checks the chosen pictures.","The control experiment tests what visual similarity itself does to naming."]},{"text":"So the effects in Experiment 1 are not likely to be due to visual similarity.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p021":[{"text":"**Hermens and colleagues** showed people several objects at the same time. They did not find the blocking effect.","children":[]},{"text":"That raises a worry.","children":["Maybe something is odd about seeing objects one after another, in serial.","If so, the blocking effect might come from the serial presentation."]},{"text":"Belke and colleagues addressed this with a new task.","children":["Participants viewed **displays of four items simultaneously**.","They produced the names in serial, one after another.","One homogeneous display held duck, snake, fish and mouse."]},{"text":"The task separates seeing from speaking.","children":["The participant can see the whole set at once.","The participant still has to select and say each name in turn."]},{"text":"Blocking still appeared in naming latency and gaze duration with these displays. So the effect does not depend on seeing objects one at a time.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p022":[{"text":"Belke and colleagues measured **eye movements** while participants named the four objects. **Eye tracking** records where the eyes are and for how long.","children":[]},{"text":"The eyes and the mouth work in a sequence.","children":["The participant looks at one object.","The eyes then move to the next object.","The name of the first object is spoken after the eyes have moved on."]},{"text":"The record splits each name into intervals.","children":["**Gaze duration (GD)** is the time spent looking at an object.","**Articulation onset (Art)** is when the speaker starts saying its name.","The **lag** is the time between the end of looking and the start of the name."]},{"text":"These intervals can be compared across HOM and HET displays.","children":[]},{"text":"So eye tracking reveals where in the sequence the semantic difference appears.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p023":[{"text":"The experiment has **two key dependent variables**. Each one tracks a different stage of naming.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gaze duration** is the time spent looking at an object.","children":["There is evidence that **lemma selection** is coordinated with eye movements.","So a change in gaze duration points to a change in lemma selection."]},{"text":"The **lag** is the time after the eyes leave an object and before its name starts.","children":["Later processes happen during the lag.","**Phonological encoding** is building the sound form of the word.","**Gesture planning** is planning the speech movements.","These can happen without visual support once the eyes have moved on."]},{"text":"The two measures split apart stages that overall naming time combines.","children":["An effect on gaze duration points to selection.","An effect on lag points to processing after the eyes move."]},{"text":"So the question is which interval the semantic manipulation changes.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p024":[{"text":"**Griffin, Bock and colleagues** made demonstrations of **eye-mouth coordination**. They track where a speaker looks while describing an event.","children":[]},{"text":"One example is accurately labeling a patient in \"The horse is kicking a cow.\"","children":["The **agent** is the one doing the action, which is the horse.","The **patient** is the one affected by the action, which is the cow."]},{"text":"The demonstrations link three things.","children":["The first is the event being described.","The second is the places the speaker looks.","The third is the words the speaker produces."]},{"text":"The eye record makes sense when it is lined up with the spoken words.","children":["It reveals when each participant in the event matters for building the sentence.","It extends object naming to a full sentence."]},{"text":"So eye-mouth coordination is a useful tool for studying speech production.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p025":[{"text":"The eye-tracking experiment produced **three measures** with different results.","children":[]},{"text":"For **overall speech duration**, times decrease over the course of the experiment.","children":["Speech duration does not show a blocking effect."]},{"text":"For **gaze duration**, there is a **blocking effect**.","children":["People look longer at objects in HOM displays.","The effect appears only after the first cycle."]},{"text":"For **lag**, there is **no effect**.","children":["Numerically, the blocking effect seems to go in the opposite direction.","That difference is not significant."]},{"text":"Practice and semantic context must be kept apart again.","children":["A measure can get shorter across cycles without differing between HOM and HET.","Only gaze duration differs reliably between HOM and HET."]},{"text":"So the semantic cost appears in the time spent looking at each object.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p026":[{"text":"Each measure maps onto a stage of production.","children":[]},{"text":"**Speech duration** maps onto the **gesture level**.","children":["It drops over the experiment but has no blocking effect."]},{"text":"**Gaze duration** maps onto **lemma selection**.","children":["It has a blocking effect after the first cycle.","Longer looking in HOM displays fits more competition among related lemmas."]},{"text":"**Lag** maps onto **phonological encoding**.","children":["It has no significant blocking effect.","So there is no evidence of a blocking cost after the eyes leave the object."]},{"text":"The conclusion rests on the whole pattern.","children":["A positive effect on gaze duration points to selection.","The other measures show no blocking, so the cost is clearest at selection."]},{"text":"So the results place semantic blocking at **lemma selection**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p027":[{"text":"**Experiment 3** asked whether blocking **generalizes to new items** in the same category. **New items** were introduced in **Cycle 5**.","children":[]},{"text":"Two things happened at Cycle 5.","children":["The repetition priming effect went away for the new items.","Overall performance slowed down dramatically."]},{"text":"The blocking effect was still there.","children":["HOM naming stayed slower than HET naming.","The new items had never been named before."]},{"text":"The new items can be compared to the items in Cycle 1.","children":["In Cycle 1, no item had been named yet, and there was no blocking.","In Cycle 5, the new items came from categories already named, and blocking appeared."]},{"text":"So yes, blocking generalizes to new items. It comes from the **semantic category**, not from repeating a particular list of names.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p028":[{"text":"Belke and colleagues isolate the blocking effect in the model by ruling out **three other sources**.","children":[]},{"text":"The first source is **visual recognition**.","children":["Related objects might look alike.","This source is ruled out."]},{"text":"The second source is **phonological encoding**.","children":["The words are not similar in sound.","So sound overlap cannot cause the slowdown."]},{"text":"The third source is an **item-specific** effect.","children":["The effect might come only from repeating particular items.","This is ruled out because blocking generalizes to new items."]},{"text":"Each control targets a different point between seeing an object and saying its name.","children":["Together the controls narrow the explanation."]},{"text":"So the remaining account is **lemma selection** among semantically activated competitors.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p029":[{"text":"Each alternative explanation is paired with its own test.","children":[]},{"text":"For **visual recognition**, there are two tests.","children":["The stimuli were normed and matched for visual similarity.","A control experiment tested visual similarity directly."]},{"text":"For **phonological encoding**, there are two tests, marked with a question mark.","children":["The words are not phonologically similar.","Gaze duration is affected, but eye-speech lag is not.","The question mark means the timing measures support an interpretation rather than directly observing the stage."]},{"text":"For **repeating specific lists**, there is one test.","children":["The effect generalizes to new items.","The new items keep the category but change the actual names."]},{"text":"The tests give complementary evidence.","children":["Generalization changes item identity.","Eye tracking splits naming into measured intervals."]},{"text":"So the effect is located around **lemma selection**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p030":[{"text":"An idea for an experiment starts with one question. The researcher asks \"Couldn't it just be …?\"","children":[]},{"text":"For **stimulus manipulations**, many features of words are correlated with **frequency**.","children":["One feature is **length**.","Another feature is **age of acquisition**.","Others are **concreteness** or **imageability**.","Another is **contextual diversity**."]},{"text":"So frequency effects might be just a catch-all for these other properties.","children":["To test this, the researcher matches a suspected feature or manipulates it directly.","Oldfield and Wingfield did this when they matched for length."]},{"text":"For **experimental manipulations**, even a simple blocking study involves many processes.","children":["One process is **object recognition**.","Another process is **response selection**.","Belke and colleagues tested such processes with controls, new items and eye tracking."]},{"text":"So a useful alternative must be specific enough to predict a different result.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p031":[],"m1-review-p001":[],"m1-review-p002":[{"text":"The **Midterm 1** exam has **ten short essay questions**. An answer of about **100 to 250 words** should be enough for each one.","children":["Each question has **three or four gradeable items**. Each item earns credit on its own, so there is lots of **partial credit**."]},{"text":"The exam is **open book**.","children":["Names and dates can be looked up during the exam, so there is no need to memorize them."]},{"text":"So the work is explaining each concept clearly with the right example and the right evidence.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p003":[],"m1-review-p004":[{"text":"The consonant chart organizes sounds by three **features**. Each feature describes one separate property of how a sound is made.","children":["**Place of articulation** is where the vocal tract closes or narrows. It runs along the horizontal axis, from the front of the vocal tract to the back.","**Manner of articulation** is the degree of closure. It runs along the vertical axis, from fully closed to open.","**Voicing** is the timing of vocal fold vibration. It separates the two sounds in each pair."]},{"text":"The two members of a voicing pair differ in when voicing starts.","children":["For the **voiced** member, voicing starts before or during the closure.","For the **voiceless** member, voicing starts after the closure."]},{"text":"Bilabial sounds such as p and b sit toward the left, because the lips are at the front. Velar sounds sit farther right, because they are made farther back.","children":[]},{"text":"So every consonant gets three answers. These are where it is made, how closed it is, and when voicing starts.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p005":[{"text":"**/p/** and **/b/** can each be described as a bundle of **features**. Features describe how sounds are alike and different in a precise, well-defined way.","children":["/p/ is plus **plosive**, plus **bilabial** and minus **voiced**.","/b/ is plus plosive, plus bilabial and plus voiced."]},{"text":"The two sounds share the same **manner** and the same **place** of articulation.","children":["Both are plosives, so the airflow is fully stopped and then released.","Both are bilabial, so the lips make the closure."]},{"text":"The two sounds differ only in **voicing**.","children":["Voicing here means the timing of **phonation** relative to the release of the closure. Phonation is vibration of the vocal folds."]},{"text":"The same difference separates **/t/** from **/d/**. That pair is made at a different place, but the voicing contrast is the same.","children":["So features make it possible to talk in general about **the voiced stops** /b/, /d/ and /g/. This general description is helpful for phonological descriptions."]}],"m1-review-p006":[{"text":"A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in only one sound and also differ in meaning. Minimal pairs are important for showing **contrast**.","children":[]},{"text":"Two **phones** are **contrastive** when swapping them changes the meaning of a word. Contrastive phones are examples of different **phonemes**.","children":[]},{"text":"The test for two different **low back vowels** works this way.","children":["You must find two words whose difference depends only on those two vowels.","For a speaker who distinguishes them, **caught** and **cot** do this job. The consonants stay the same, so the vowel carries the difference.","For a speaker who merges these vowels, caught and cot do not show two separate phonemes."]},{"text":"So the evidence for a phoneme is a change in meaning. A sound difference alone is not enough.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p007":[{"text":"Minimal pairs also show which **feature** a contrast depends on.","children":[]},{"text":"In English, three stop pairs all differ in **voicing**.","children":["**/b/ and /p/** differ in voicing. Both are made at the lips.","**/t/ and /d/** differ in voicing. Both are made further back, at the alveolar place.","**/k/ and /g/** differ in voicing. Both are made at the velar place."]},{"text":"Within each pair, place and manner stay the same. Across the pairs, the place changes.","children":["So the same voicing contrast repeats at three different places."]},{"text":"The evidence comes from comparing minimal pairs in each language.","children":["A contrast found in English does not automatically hold in another language."]},{"text":"So voicing is one general feature that separates many English words.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p008":[{"text":"Phonemes are **not letters**. Literate people easily imagine speech as an orderly stream of letter-like units. Speech is nothing like that.","children":[]},{"text":"A **spectrogram** displays the sound of speech over time.","children":["Time runs from left to right.","Frequency rises from bottom to top.","Dark regions mean strong acoustic energy."]},{"text":"The spectrogram of \"A sore back is a real pain\" has no clean breaks between sounds.","children":["Neighboring sounds blend into each other through smooth transitions.","The same phoneme does not have one fixed acoustic shape in every context."]},{"text":"So a transcription is a row of separate symbols, while the speech signal is **continuous**. That is why phonemes cannot be treated as letters lined up in the sound.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p009":[{"text":"**Hockett (1955)** described speech with a homely analogy about **Easter eggs**. He called it the best analogy he could think of.","children":["A row of raw eggs of various sizes and colors rides along a moving belt.","The belt carries the eggs through the two rollers of a **wringer**. The wringer smashes them and rubs them into each other."]},{"text":"Each part of the analogy stands for part of speech.","children":["The flow of eggs before the wringer is the series of impulses from the **phoneme source**.","The mess that comes out is the output of the **speech transmitter**.","An **inspector** examines the mess and decides which eggs went in. The inspector is the **hearer**."]},{"text":"The inspector does not have to put the eggs back together. That would be physically impossible.","children":["He only has to **identify** the eggs. He uses the broken yolks, the spread-out whites and the bits of colored shell."]},{"text":"So a listener identifies the intended phonemes from a smeared, overlapping signal. The listener never has to recover separate pieces.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p010":[],"m1-review-p011":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology** derives new words from existing parts. These parts are called **morphemes**.","children":[]},{"text":"The word **morphology** is itself built this way.","children":["**morph-** comes from Greek and means \"form\".","**-ology** also comes from Greek and means something like \"the study of\".","Together they make \"the study of the forms of words\"."]},{"text":"A similar process gives the word **morpheme**. It also gives **phoneme**.","children":["**Phone** is another Greek morpheme, probably meaning \"speech\"."]},{"text":"The pattern does not always work smoothly.","children":["Following it for units in text leads to **graphology**, which is an awkward result."]},{"text":"So a complex word is built from reusable parts, but the parts do not always predict the meaning.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p012":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology** is **productive**, but only in limited ways. Affixes differ in how widely and how regularly they are applied.","children":[]},{"text":"The suffix **-hood** added to a noun stem means a state of being linked to that noun.","children":["For **brotherhood** and **sisterhood**, the pattern works as expected.","For **knighthood**, the result is a bit odd. Two brothers share their brotherhood, but each knight has his own knighthood.","For **neighborhood**, the word is perfectly good, but -hood does a different job entirely.","For **friendhood**, there is no word. English says friendship instead, for no apparent reason.","For **enemyhood**, there is no word either. The concept only has formal words like \"enmity\" or \"antagonism\"."]},{"text":"Some of these examples come from **Liberman and Prince**.","children":[]},{"text":"So an affix does not apply to every stem it could fit. Knowing an affix means knowing where it applies and what job it does.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p013":[{"text":"**Zero derivation** changes a word's category without adding any affix. Even so, it does complicated things with meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"The nouns **dish**, **spoon**, **fork** and **table** are strong associates of the noun **knife**. As verbs or in fixed phrases, their meanings are not similar.","children":["In \"She can dish it out but she can't take it\", dish is a verb that has nothing to do with plates.","In \"He was born with a silver spoon and a forked tongue in his mouth\", spoon and fork appear in fixed figurative phrases.","In \"They decided to table the issue after a day of inconclusive negotiations\", table is a verb about handling an issue."]},{"text":"So the answer is no. Each converted word has its own conventional meaning, which has to be learned.","children":[]},{"text":"Figurative phrases like silver spoon are studied in **Cognitive Linguistics**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p014":[{"text":"Derivational morphology more commonly **changes the pronunciation** and the spelling of a word. Adding an affix can change the sounds in the rest of the word.","children":[]},{"text":"The **certificate** family changes its **stress** with each affix.","children":["In certificate /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/, the main stress falls on the second syllable.","In certification /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/, the main stress moves to the fourth syllable. The second vowel also changes from I to ɨ.","In recertification /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/, the main stress moves to the first syllable."]},{"text":"This is one reason for **\"The Chaos\"** of English spelling.","children":["It is also why spelling closer to the spoken form does not necessarily improve readability."]},{"text":"Some noun and verb pairs differ mainly in **stress**.","children":["**record** is one such pair.","**invite** is another, as in \"He did not receive an invite\" and \"We did not invite him\".","**entrance** also changes stress, but its noun and verb are not actually related to each other."]},{"text":"So morphology and pronunciation interact, and stress is often the main change.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p015":[{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** changes the meaning of words in a more **predictable** way than derivation. It adds grammatical information to the same word.","children":[]},{"text":"For **plurals**, the noun marks number.","children":["knife becomes knives, one fork becomes two forks, and one mouse becomes a lot of mice."]},{"text":"For the **third person singular present**, the verb marks agreement.","children":["knife as a verb becomes knifes. I walk becomes she walks. You run becomes he runs."]},{"text":"For the **past**, the verb marks tense.","children":["knife as a verb becomes knifed. Today I walk becomes yesterday I walked. Today she sings becomes in the past, she sang."]},{"text":"**Menn** says we would think it silly to have separate dictionary entries for singular and plural nouns.","children":[]},{"text":"The sound changes of inflection often have some **irregularity**, as in mice and sang.","children":["This irregularity matters for **language learning models**. It also matters for kinds of **developmental language impairment**."]}],"m1-review-p016":[{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** also depends on the **syntax** of the rest of the sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"The phrase \"two [noun]+pl\" has a slot for a noun. Whatever noun goes there must be pluralized somehow.","children":["For fork, the plural is regular, so it becomes two forks.","For knife, the plural is irregular, so it becomes two knives."]},{"text":"Derivation has no syntactic process like this, even for very productive affixes.","children":["The closest case is a formula like \"Once you [verb] it, you can un-[verb] it\".","That formula is not exactly syntax, so it does not force a derived form."]},{"text":"So syntax can require an inflected form. It does not require a derived form. This difference matters for models of **speech production**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p017":[],"m1-review-p018":[{"text":"A simple model treats words as **nodes** in a **lexical network**. Each node is a lexical representation that a specific word can activate.","children":[]},{"text":"The example network has three nodes.","children":["The nodes are **lion**, **tiger** and **stripes**.","The input is the printed word **LION**."]},{"text":"The input and the nodes are different levels.","children":["The printed word supplies perceptual evidence.","The node stands for the word the system could recognize from that evidence."]},{"text":"So recognizing a word means activating its matching node.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p019":[{"text":"**Activation** can be **partial**. A node can be partly active before the word is recognized.","children":[]},{"text":"Here, **LION** contrasts poorly with a **noise mask**.","children":["The word is hard to separate from the noise, so the input is weak evidence.","The lion node therefore gets only **weak activation**.","This activation is not yet enough for the word to be identified."]},{"text":"So the state of the network changes gradually as evidence comes in. It does not jump straight from off to recognized.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p020":[{"text":"The **signal-to-noise ratio (SNR)** compares the strength of the word with the strength of the background noise.","children":[]},{"text":"At some point, the SNR becomes very favorable.","children":["Activity in the lion node becomes strong.","At that point the simple network has **\"identified\"** the word lion.","The tiger and stripes nodes stay less active, so they are not identified."]},{"text":"This is a model of the **perceptual identification task**.","children":["Participants push a button as soon as they can identify the item.","The measure is how long identification takes."]},{"text":"So a faster button press means the node reached strong activation sooner.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p021":[{"text":"Most models of this kind assume that **activation unfolds over time**. A word's activation rises after the input arrives and then falls.","children":[]},{"text":"These models explain **frequency effects**, where frequent words are identified faster. One explanation is that experience tunes the **activation dynamics**.","children":["Each encounter might raise the word's **baseline** level of activation. The word then starts closer to recognition.","Each encounter might strengthen its **incoming connections**. The word can then be activated more quickly.","Each encounter might change a **threshold** parameter. The word then needs a different amount of activation to be recognized."]},{"text":"This is a very simple form of **learning**.","children":[]},{"text":"Different computational models simulate frequency effects in different ways.","children":["One example is **Rumelhart and McClelland's (1986) Interactive Activation model**."]},{"text":"So frequent words are faster because past experience has changed how their activation behaves.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p022":[{"text":"Activation dynamics also explain **repetition priming**. Seeing a word again very soon after seeing it leads to faster identification.","children":[]},{"text":"Activation does not rise and fall instantly.","children":["One assumption is that activation **falls more slowly than it rises**.","So some activation is still left over after the first exposure."]},{"text":"Suppose the word is encountered again while activation is still high.","children":["Its **baseline** is then already higher than usual.","It needs less new activation to reach identification."]},{"text":"So the repeated word is identified faster because it starts from leftover activation.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p023":[{"text":"The simple network leaves out **two factors**.","children":["**Neighborhood effects** depend on how similar a word is to other words.","**Associative, semantic and phonological priming** depend on whether you just saw a similar or related word."]},{"text":"Both factors involve a second word. A network of separate nodes can only change each word's own activation.","children":[]},{"text":"So the network needs something added. It needs **connections** between nodes. Those connections let one word's activation affect another word.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p024":[{"text":"**Spreading activation** is one way to represent word associations. The associations come from word association **norms**.","children":[]},{"text":"The model is a network of nodes. The **connection strengths**, or the distances, between nodes are set by their association.","children":["When you activate lion, some of that activation travels along a weighted connection to tiger.","A strong association passes more activation than a weak one."]},{"text":"In the example network, vehicle connects to car and to bus. Fire engine connects to the vehicles and to fire-related concepts.","children":["So one active concept can partly activate several related concepts."]},{"text":"That is why a related word, already partly active, becomes easier to process. This benefit is **semantic priming**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p025":[{"text":"**Balota and Lorch** tested whether activation spreads beyond directly associated concepts.","children":[]},{"text":"They built prime and target pairs related only through a **mediating concept**.","children":["The prime **lion** and the target **stripes** are linked only through **tiger**.","They compared directly related pairs, pairs linked through a mediating concept, a neutral prime and an unrelated prime."]},{"text":"They used two tasks.","children":["In **lexical decision**, people judged whether the target was a word. Directly related pairs such as lion and tiger sped up responses. Lion before stripes did not help compared with neutral or unrelated primes.","In **pronunciation**, people read the target aloud. Both directly related primes and the lion before stripes primes sped up responses."]},{"text":"They concluded that activation **spreads beyond directly related concepts** in semantic memory.","children":["They suggested that features of the lexical decision task **masked** the indirect priming effect."]}],"m1-review-p026":[{"text":"**Indirect priming** happens when a prime helps a target through a **mediating** word.","children":[]},{"text":"In the network, lion connects to tiger. Tiger connects to stripes. Lion and stripes are not directly associated.","children":["Activating lion sends activation to tiger.","Tiger then passes some of that activation on to stripes."]},{"text":"The idea is that activity **flows throughout the network**.","children":["So activating lion can prime stripes, even with no direct link between them."]},{"text":"That is why indirect priming is evidence for spreading activation.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p027":[{"text":"The indirect priming result is very representative of psycholinguistics.","children":["It needed **careful study design**.","The effects are **small**.","The result is **counterintuitive**."]},{"text":"In Experiment 2, **Balota and Lorch** measured **pronunciation latencies**. **Stimulus onset asynchrony (SOA)** is the time between the start of the prime and the start of the target. They used SOAs of 250 ms and 500 ms.","children":["Averaged over both SOAs, related primes gave 549 ms.","Primes linked through tiger gave 558 ms.","Neutral primes gave 577 ms, and unrelated primes gave 575 ms."]},{"text":"The indirect advantage is only 17 to 19 ms. It still appears at both SOAs.","children":[]},{"text":"Priming between stripes and lion seems strange because they have no direct link.","children":["The only route between them goes through tiger.","So the effect only makes sense if activation spreads through the network."]}],"m1-review-p028":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues (2005)** used the **WEAVER++** model of word production. The model has a **conceptual stratum** and a **lemma stratum**.","children":["In the conceptual stratum, duck, fish, snake and mouse connect to shared nodes such as animal, bird, swim and scales.","Each concept connects down to its **lemma**. A lemma is the word's entry in the syntactic stratum."]},{"text":"A naive view of spreading activation predicts **faster** naming. A block of similar pictures should prime one another.","children":[]},{"text":"The model explains how spreading activation can lead to **slower** naming instead.","children":["Naming requires **selecting** one lemma.","Similar pictures activate many related lemmas at once, so the lemmas compete."]},{"text":"So more activation does not always help. Selection gets harder when competitors are active.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p029":[],"m1-review-p030":[{"text":"**Pierre Paul Broca** was a neurologist. In **1861** he presented the case of a patient called **\"Tan\"**.","children":["Tan had lost the ability to speak after a brain injury decades earlier.","Broca presented the case to the newly founded **Society of Anthropology**. He was also its founding president."]},{"text":"Tan showed a split between speaking and understanding.","children":["For **speech**, Tan could only produce one jargon syllable.","For his **other abilities**, he seemed to be \"in possession of his senses\".","For **comprehension**, he seemed to understand what was being said."]},{"text":"Tan was not the first observed case of a specific loss like this.","children":[]},{"text":"So a brain injury can remove speech while leaving understanding in place. That made the case central to the **localization** of language.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p031":[{"text":"Tan's brain is on display at the **Musee Dupuytren**.","children":[]},{"text":"The anatomical description reports a **large lesion**.","children":["The lesion definitely includes the third convolution of the frontal lobe. This is the **inferior frontal gyrus**."]},{"text":"This region became known as **Broca's area**. It is a portion of the left frontal lobe that is important for speech.","children":[]},{"text":"**Hughlings Jackson (1874)** criticized this reasoning at the time.","children":["He said that \"to locate the damage which destroys speech and to locate speech are two different things\".","Damage in one place can stop speech without that place holding speech itself."]},{"text":"So a lesion shows where damage disrupts speech, not where speech itself is located.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p032":[{"text":"In the **1870s**, German neurologists such as **Lichtheim** and **Wernicke** made more systematic observations of **aphasia**. Aphasia is a loss of language ability after brain damage.","children":[]},{"text":"They explained specific losses of function as **disconnections** between **centers**.","children":["In Lichtheim's house diagram, A is the center for sound images. B is the center for concepts. M is the center for movement images.","A disconnection between A and B could cause a loss of understanding spoken words.","A disconnection between A and M could cause a loss of verbatim repetition."]},{"text":"This approach of describing centers for specific functions is an ancestor of **modularity**.","children":[]},{"text":"Eling and Whitaker (2009) review this history.","children":[]},{"text":"So the explanation covers the pathways between centers, not only the centers themselves.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p033":[{"text":"**Sigmund Freud** criticized the **\"diagram-drawing\" school** of aphasia research. He did this in his early neurological research.","children":[]},{"text":"Freud thought it naive to expect the brain to follow psychological categories.","children":["Examples of such categories are \"word forms\" and \"concepts\".","He proposed instead that language emerged from **fields**.","These fields connect perceptual and motor abilities to **drives**."]},{"text":"This is a **holist critique**.","children":[]},{"text":"It names a durable tension in **cognitive neuroscience**.","children":["One question is whether brain area functions can be directly related to cognitive functions.","Another question is whether the brain is really organized by our descriptions of cognitive phenomena."]},{"text":"So a useful psychological category does not automatically match a brain area. The link has to be shown with evidence.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p034":[{"text":"**Dronkers and colleagues** used **MRI** to assess Broca's landmark cases very precisely. One of these brains belonged to **Leborgne**, the patient known as Tan.","children":[]},{"text":"The MRI shows the real extent and location of the damage.","children":["The lesion is very **deep** and includes the **insula**.","There is a lot of **white matter damage** to the **superior longitudinal fasciculus**. This pathway connects the front and back of the cerebral cortex."]},{"text":"Other work by this group links **speech apraxia** strongly to the insula, not to Broca's area.","children":["Speech apraxia is the condition Broca described in his patients."]},{"text":"So the damage was much larger than Broca's area alone. That is why the speech loss may come from the insula rather than Broca's area.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p035":[],"m1-review-p036":[{"text":"The **functional level** selects **lemmas**, not finished words. A lemma is a word's identity before its grammatical form is fixed.","children":["The term **lemma** is borrowed from mathematics. There it refers to a proposition in an argument."]},{"text":"One speech error explains why the functional level cannot just select words.","children":["The intended sentence was \"Where'd I put it when I brought it in\".","The speaker said \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in\"."]},{"text":"The verbs switched places, but the tense was applied after the switch.","children":["If the word \"brought\" had been selected, it would have moved as \"brought\".","Instead it came out as \"bring\", the form its new position needed."]},{"text":"So the speaker selected the lemma **BRING**. It was expressed in its present form only later. That is why selection works on lemmas, not words.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p037":[{"text":"People often say the **opposite** of what they mean because **antonyms** are similar at many levels.","children":[]},{"text":"Antonyms overlap in meaning and association.","children":["They are **semantically related**.","They are **strongly associated**.","In the **spreading activation** framework, these two links together make a perfect storm."]},{"text":"Antonyms also fit the same grammar.","children":["They can take on the same **semantic roles**.","They belong to the **same word class**, so they can take on the same syntactic roles.","So the rest of the speech production system can handle either one."]},{"text":"So activation spreads easily from a **lemma** to its antonym. That is why the wrong antonym gets said and still sounds grammatical.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p038":[{"text":"**Semantic roles**, also called functional roles, describe how each participant relates to an event. They stay the same when the sentence form changes.","children":[]},{"text":"In \"The plums in the icebox were eaten by me\", the order is **Undergoer**, **Location**, Verb, **Agent**.","children":["The plums are the undergoer. The icebox is the location. The speaker is the agent."]},{"text":"In \"I have eaten the plums in the icebox\", the order is Agent, Verb, Undergoer, Location.","children":["The same participants keep the same roles."]},{"text":"This simple event structure lets lemmas be slotted into roles.","children":["The verb is TO EAT. The agent is ME. PLUM is the undergoer."]},{"text":"The order of the roles has **syntactic consequences**.","children":["The order decides how the lemma ME turns into a word, either I or me.","That decision happens at the **positional level**."]},{"text":"So roles are set at the functional level, and word forms come later.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p039":[{"text":"By the end of the **functional level**, three things are done.","children":["A **message** to convey has been decided.","Appropriate **lemmas** have been selected.","The lemmas have been associated with **semantic roles**."]},{"text":"This is quite a lot of work. Yet the speaker is nowhere near putting a clause together.","children":["The word order is not set yet.","The word forms are not set yet.","Nothing has been said out loud."]},{"text":"So the functional level produces a plan of meaning, not a sentence.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p040":[{"text":"The **positional level** expresses the functional plan in syntax. The sentence \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in?\" works as the example.","children":[]},{"text":"After the functional level, the speaker knows three things.","children":["There are two verbs, **PUT** and **BRING**.","I/ME is the **agent** of both verbs.","IT is the **undergoer** of both verbs."]},{"text":"The speaker still needs more.","children":["The speaker needs the **temporal relationship** between the verbs. The putting in happened in the past, so its clause needs a past form.","The speaker needs the question structure."]},{"text":"Expressing all of these things syntactically is the job of the positional level.","children":[]},{"text":"So knowing who did what is different from knowing where each word goes.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p041":[{"text":"The **positional level** builds a **syntactic structure** with **slots**. The slots hold the lemmas from the functional level and the other particles the grammar requires.","children":[]},{"text":"Without the question and tense steps, the frame is \"Where'd I [verb, root form] it when I [verb, past] it in?\"","children":["Getting the first clause right takes a lot of syntactic work. It needs **Wh- movement** and an **auxiliary verb** to form the question.","The first clause requires the **base form** of the lemma.","The next clause requires the **past form**."]},{"text":"The error \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in\" arises while these slots are **filled with lemmas**.","children":["BRING went into the first slot, so it took the base form.","PUT went into the past slot, and its past form is also put."]},{"text":"So the slot decides the form of the verb. The lemma only decides which verb it is.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p042":[{"text":"\"A language needer learns\" is a similar speech error.","children":[]},{"text":"It is similar to the bring and put error.","children":["Two lemmas got switched when they were being fed into the positional level.","The positional level itself stayed almost the same."]},{"text":"It is different in the **noun**.","children":["The noun is really a **verb plus an affix**. Here the verb need gets the affix -er.","Adding -er is **derivational morphology**.","The verb slot keeps its **inflectional** ending, so learn becomes learns."]},{"text":"The roots moved, but the affixes stayed in their slots.","children":[]},{"text":"So derivational affixes can belong to the positional frame too, not only to the lemma.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p043":[{"text":"\"He did that easy enoughly\" is another similar error.","children":[]},{"text":"It is similar to \"a language needer learns\".","children":["Both have a **stranded affix**. A stranded affix stays in its slot while the root changes.","Here the affix is **-ly**, which makes an adjective into an adverb."]},{"text":"It is different because something also happened when **setting up the positional level**.","children":["The intended adjective phrase has an adverb first. That adverb is built as an adjective plus -ly. An adjective comes second.","The error structure reverses this order. The plain adjective comes first, and the adjective plus -ly comes second.","So easy fills the plain adjective slot, and enough gets the -ly."]},{"text":"So this error involves the frame itself, not only the lemmas filling it.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p044":[],"m1-review-p045":[{"text":"**Oldfield and Wingfield (1965)** asked people to **name pictures** of objects.","children":[]},{"text":"They found a **log-linear relationship** between frequency and response time.","children":["More frequent names were produced faster.","Response time fell in a straight line as the logarithm of frequency rose."]},{"text":"The line drawings used in picture naming come from **Snodgrass and Vanderwart (1980)**.","children":[]},{"text":"Frequency is related to **word length**, even in this tiny sample.","children":["Frequent words tend to be shorter.","So length is a **confound**. A confound is a second variable that changes along with the one being studied.","Either frequency or length could explain the naming times."]},{"text":"That is why this result is a classic cautionary tale.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p046":[{"text":"English **word frequency** drops off very fast from the top words. This pattern is **Zipf's law** in action.","children":[]},{"text":"The 200 most frequent words in an old corpus were sorted into ten bins of twenty words each.","children":["For the **first bin**, mean frequency is above 15,000 per million.","For the **second bin**, it drops below 4,000 per million.","For the later bins, it falls close to zero."]},{"text":"**Length** goes the other way.","children":["Words in the first bin average under three letters.","Words in the last bins average about five letters.","So length is **inversely related** to frequency."]},{"text":"The top twenty words include the, of, and, to and a. Each has three letters or fewer. Later high-frequency words such as century and Washington are much longer.","children":[]},{"text":"So choosing frequent words also means choosing short words.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p047":[{"text":"High-frequency (**HF**) words are generally **shorter** than low-frequency (**LF**) words. So a length difference always appears if LF words are selected at random.","children":[]},{"text":"The solution is **not to select LF words at random**.","children":["Choose long-ish HF words.","Choose short-ish LF words.","Then the two groups can be **matched on length** while frequency is manipulated."]},{"text":"So the groups differ in frequency but not in length. That separates the two explanations.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p048":[{"text":"**Oldfield and Wingfield** eventually **matched** their high and low frequency words for **length**.","children":[]},{"text":"If length caused the effect, matching should have removed it.","children":["They still found a **robust frequency effect**.","So they concluded that frequency effects are not merely length effects in disguise."]},{"text":"A lot of psycholinguistics tries to show that an observed effect is not just an effect of a **confounding variable**.","children":[]},{"text":"That is why careful stimulus control matters.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p049":[],"m1-review-p050":[{"text":"**Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005)** studied **cyclic semantic blocking** in **picture naming**.","children":[]},{"text":"In this paradigm, people repeatedly name **sets** of objects.","children":["In **homogeneous sets**, the names are semantically related.","In **heterogeneous sets**, the names are unrelated.","Naming latencies are usually longer in related sets. This cost is the **blocking effect**."]},{"text":"They ran three experiments.","children":["In **Experiment 1**, the effect only arose after all objects of a set had been shown and named once.","In **Experiment 2**, the objects of a set were shown together. Blocking appeared in naming latencies and in **gaze durations**.","The **gaze-to-speech lag** is the time from the eyes leaving an object to the start of saying its name. It showed repetition priming but no blocking.","In **Experiment 3**, blocking generalized to new, previously unnamed items."]},{"text":"They proposed that the blocking effect comes from **refractory behaviour** in the semantic system.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p051":[{"text":"Speech production must separate **activation** from **selection**.","children":[]},{"text":"Spreading activation from **duck** to **fish** means that activating one helps activate the other.","children":["This causes problems when the task is to **select** the correct **lemma** from many active ones."]},{"text":"The **WEAVER++** model sets **selection time** by a **ratio**. The ratio compares the activity of the most active lemma with the overall activation of all lemmas.","children":["When the target stands far above the rest, selection is fast.","When many lemmas are active, the target stands out less, so selection is slower."]},{"text":"**Feedback from the category level** creates **collateral activation** for items from the same category.","children":[]},{"text":"So naming a duck among other animals slows down lemma selection. That is the **semantic blocking effect**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p052":[{"text":"**Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005)** studied **cyclic semantic blocking** in **picture naming**.","children":[]},{"text":"In this paradigm, people repeatedly name **sets** of objects.","children":["In **homogeneous sets**, the names are semantically related.","In **heterogeneous sets**, the names are unrelated.","Naming latencies are usually longer in related sets. This cost is the **blocking effect**."]},{"text":"They ran three experiments.","children":["In **Experiment 1**, the effect only arose after all objects of a set had been shown and named once.","In **Experiment 2**, the objects of a set were shown together. Blocking appeared in naming latencies and in **gaze durations**.","The **gaze-to-speech lag** is the time from the eyes leaving an object to the start of saying its name. It showed repetition priming but no blocking.","In **Experiment 3**, blocking generalized to new, previously unnamed items."]},{"text":"They proposed that the blocking effect comes from **refractory behaviour** in the semantic system.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p053":[{"text":"Speech production must separate **activation** from **selection**.","children":[]},{"text":"Spreading activation from **duck** to **fish** means that activating one helps activate the other.","children":["This causes problems when the task is to **select** the correct **lemma** from many active ones."]},{"text":"The **WEAVER++** model sets **selection time** by a **ratio**. The ratio compares the activity of the most active lemma with the overall activation of all lemmas.","children":["When the target stands far above the rest, selection is fast.","When many lemmas are active, the target stands out less, so selection is slower."]},{"text":"**Feedback from the category level** creates **collateral activation** for items from the same category.","children":[]},{"text":"So naming a duck among other animals slows down lemma selection. That is the **semantic blocking effect**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p054":[{"text":"In **Experiment 2**, Belke, Meyer and Damian measured three timings for each named object. Each timing is linked to one stage of production.","children":[]},{"text":"For **speech duration**, overall duration decreases over the experiment. It shows **no blocking effect**.","children":["Speech duration reflects the **gesture level**."]},{"text":"For **gaze duration**, there is a **blocking effect**, but only after the first cycle. Objects in homogeneous sets are looked at longer.","children":["Gaze duration reflects **lemma selection**."]},{"text":"For **lag**, there is **no effect**. Numerically, the blocking effect seems to go the opposite way, but this is not significant.","children":["Lag reflects **phonological encoding**."]},{"text":"So blocking appears only in the measure for lemma selection. That places the effect at **lemma selection**.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p055":[{"text":"**Experiment 3** asked whether **blocking generalizes** to new items in the same category. The answer is yes.","children":[]},{"text":"New items were introduced in **Cycle 5**.","children":["Overall naming slows down dramatically for both kinds of sets.","The **repetition priming** effect goes away in Cycle 5, because these items have not been named before.","The **blocking effect** is still there. Homogeneous sets stay slower than heterogeneous sets."]},{"text":"The useful comparison is between new items from a blocked category and the items in **Cycle 1**.","children":["In Cycle 1, no category had been named yet, and there was no blocking.","In Cycle 5, the items are new but their category has been named before, and blocking appears."]},{"text":"So blocking comes from the named **category**, not from repeating the same items.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p056":[{"text":"Belke and colleagues isolated the blocking effect in the model by ruling out three other explanations.","children":[]},{"text":"The effect is **not visual recognition**.","children":["The stimuli were **normed and matched**.","A **control experiment** checked the visual side."]},{"text":"The effect is probably **not phonological encoding**. This part is marked with a question mark, so it is less certain.","children":["The words in a set are **not phonologically similar**.","**Gaze duration** shows the effect, but the **eye-speech lag** does not."]},{"text":"The effect is **not just a specific effect of repeating lists**.","children":["It **generalizes to new items**."]},{"text":"So the blocking effect is placed at **lemma selection**.","children":[]}]}};
